window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-cbse12-acc-debentures","kind":"practice","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"title":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures · Practice","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","subject":"Accountancy","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-001","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":1,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. acquired assets of ₹4,20,000 and took over liabilities of ₹50,000 from Kapoor Bros. for a purchase consideration of ₹3,42,000. The consideration was paid by issuing 12% debentures of ₹100 each at a 5% discount. Consider the following statements about the books of Neel Ltd.:\n(i) Capital Reserve of ₹28,000 is credited.\n(ii) 3,600 debentures are issued to Kapoor Bros.\n(iii) Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited with ₹18,000.\n(iv) 12% Debentures A/c is credited with ₹3,42,000.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Net assets = 4,20,000 − 50,000 = ₹3,70,000.\n(i) Correct: consideration is less than net assets, so Capital Reserve = 3,70,000 − 3,42,000 = ₹28,000.\n(ii) Correct: issue price = ₹95, so 3,42,000 ÷ 95 = 3,600 debentures.\n(iii) Correct: discount = 3,600 × ₹5 = ₹18,000.\n(iv) Incorrect: Debentures A/c is always credited at nominal value, 3,600 × 100 = ₹3,60,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-002","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":2,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. issued 5,000, 8% debentures of Rs. 100 each at Rs. 95 per debenture. It also has equity share capital of Rs. 10,00,000. During the year, the company earned a profit before interest of only Rs. 30,000. The board therefore decided not to declare any dividend. What amount of debenture interest for the year will be debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss?","image":"","options":["Rs. 38,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 30,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 5,000 debentures of face value Rs. 100 at 8%, issued at Rs. 95; profit before interest Rs. 30,000.\nInterest is calculated on the nominal (face) value, not on the issue price. It is a charge, so it is payable even if profit is inadequate.\nFormula: Interest = Number of debentures × Face value × Rate.\nSubstituting: 5,000 × 100 × 8% = Rs. 40,000.\nSo Rs. 40,000 is debited, even though it exceeds the profit of Rs. 30,000.\nRs. 38,000 wrongly uses the issue price.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-003","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":3,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Ritu Ltd. purchased the business of Vikas Enterprises:\n- Building ₹3,00,000\n- Machinery ₹2,00,000\n- Stock ₹1,00,000\n- Creditors taken over ₹80,000\nThe purchase consideration was ₹5,80,000. Of this, ₹40,000 was paid in cash and the balance by issuing 9% debentures of ₹100 each at a premium of 20%. What amount will be credited to Securities Premium Reserve A/c?","image":"","options":["₹1,16,000","₹1,08,000","₹90,000","₹1,20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: net assets = 6,00,000 − 80,000 = ₹5,20,000. The consideration of ₹5,80,000 exceeds this, so goodwill = ₹60,000 (debited in the purchase entry).\nAmount to be settled in debentures = 5,80,000 − 40,000 = ₹5,40,000.\nIssue price = ₹120, so number of debentures = 5,40,000 ÷ 120 = 4,500.\nPremium = 4,500 × ₹20 = ₹90,000.\nOption (2) wrongly takes 20% of ₹5,40,000, but the premium is 20% of face value, not of the issue price.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-004","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":4,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Pinnacle Ltd. took a bank loan of Rs. 8,00,000. It handed over 9,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each to the bank as collateral security. The company follows the first method of accounting for such an issue. Which treatment is correct?","image":"","options":["Debenture Suspense A/c is debited and 9% Debentures A/c is credited with Rs. 9,00,000","No journal entry is passed; a note below the bank loan states that it is secured by the issue of debentures as collateral security","Bank A/c is debited and 9% Debentures A/c is credited with Rs. 9,00,000","Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited with Rs. 1,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under the first method, the issue of debentures as collateral security creates no fresh liability, so no journal entry is made.\nInstead, a note is appended below the loan in the notes to accounts, saying the loan is secured by debentures issued as collateral security.\nThe Debenture Suspense entry in option (1) belongs to the second method, which makes it the tempting wrong choice.\nNo cash is received, so a Bank debit is wrong. No loss arises either.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-005","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":5,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Shyam Ltd. purchased machinery from Kiran Traders. The purchase consideration was paid by issuing 10% debentures at par. Which journal entry records the issue of debentures to Kiran Traders?","image":"","options":["Machinery A/c Dr. To 10% Debentures A/c","Kiran Traders Dr. To 10% Debentures A/c","10% Debentures A/c Dr. To Kiran Traders","Kiran Traders Dr. To Bank A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: On purchase, Machinery A/c is debited and Kiran Traders (the vendor) is credited. The vendor is now a creditor of the company.\nStep 2: When debentures are issued at par to settle this amount, the vendor's account is debited and 10% Debentures A/c is credited.\nStep 3: Option (1) skips the vendor's account entirely. Option (4) shows a cash payment, but no cash is paid here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-006","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":6,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A company having surplus funds may prefer to redeem its debentures by purchasing them in the open market when they are quoted below their face value.\nReason (R): When own debentures are bought below nominal value and cancelled, the company pays less than the liability discharged, and the difference is a profit on redemption.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes two advantages of open market purchase: the company can redeem whenever it has surplus funds, and it can buy when debentures are available at a discount.\nR is true. The entry is Debentures A/c Dr. (nominal value) To Bank A/c (price paid) To Profit on Redemption of Debentures A/c. This profit is later transferred to Capital Reserve.\nR explains why buying at a discount is attractive, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-007","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":7,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements carefully.\nAssertion (A): A company must pay interest on its debentures even in a year in which it suffers a loss.\nReason (R): Interest on debentures is a charge against the profits of the company, not an appropriation of profits.","image":"","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is true, but (R) is false.","(A) is false, but (R) is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Debenture interest is a fixed obligation payable at the agreed rate, whether or not the company has earned a profit.\nR is true. Interest on debentures is a charge against profit. Dividend on shares, by contrast, is an appropriation of profit.\nBecause interest is a charge and not an appropriation, it does not depend on profits being available. So R correctly explains why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-008","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":8,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are methods of redemption of debentures as described in NCERT?\n(i) Payment in lump sum\n(ii) Payment in instalments by draw of lots\n(iii) Purchase in the open market for cancellation\n(iv) Conversion into shares or new debentures\n(v) Issue of debentures as collateral security\n(vi) Transfer of the balance of Debenture Redemption Reserve to General Reserve","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four methods of redemption: lump sum payment (i), payment in instalments (ii), purchase in the open market (iii), and conversion into shares or new debentures (iv).\n(v) Issuing debentures as collateral security is a way of securing a loan. It does not repay any debenture.\n(vi) Transferring DRR to General Reserve is an entry made after redemption. It is not itself a method of redemption.\nSo 4 items qualify.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-009","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":9,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Vertex Ltd. purchased its own 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each, with a total face value of Rs. 40,000, from the open market at Rs. 97.50 per debenture for immediate cancellation. It also paid brokerage and other expenses of Rs. 200 on the purchase. What amount will finally be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,000","Rs. 800","Rs. 1,200","Rs. 39,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Face value = Rs. 40,000, so the number of debentures = 400.\nStep 1: Price paid = 400 × 97.50 = Rs. 39,000. Adding expenses of Rs. 200, total cost = Rs. 39,200.\nStep 2: Profit on redemption = 40,000 − 39,200 = Rs. 800.\nStep 3: Entries: Debentures A/c Dr. 40,000, To Bank 39,200, To Profit on Redemption 800. Then Profit on Redemption A/c Dr. To Capital Reserve 800.\nRs. 1,000 is wrong because it ignores the expenses.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-010","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":10,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (terms of issue and redemption) with List-II (account(s) credited at allotment, other than Debentures A/c).\nList-I:\nA. Issued at premium, redeemable at par\nB. Issued at par, redeemable at premium\nC. Issued at premium, redeemable at premium\nD. Issued at discount, redeemable at par\nList-II:\n1. No other account is credited\n2. Securities Premium Reserve A/c only\n3. Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c only\n4. Both Securities Premium Reserve A/c and Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c","image":"","options":["A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1","A-2, B-3, C-1, D-4","A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: The premium on issue is credited to Securities Premium Reserve. Redemption is at par, so nothing else is credited. A-2.\nB: The premium payable on redemption is credited to Premium on Redemption A/c. B-3.\nC: Both the issue premium and the redemption premium are credited. C-4.\nD: The discount is debited to Discount on Issue A/c. Only Debentures A/c is credited. D-1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-011","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":11,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Type of debenture) with List-II (Feature):\nList-I\n(A) Registered debentures\n(B) Zero coupon rate debentures\n(C) Perpetual debentures\n(D) Secured debentures\nList-II\n(i) Repayable on winding-up or after a long period\n(ii) A charge is created on the company's assets\n(iii) Holders' names and particulars are entered in a register kept by the company\n(iv) Issued at a substantial discount with no stated rate of interest","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Registered debentures: holder details are kept in the company's register, so (iii).\n(B) Zero coupon debentures: no stated rate; issued at a deep discount, so (iv).\n(C) Perpetual debentures: no repayment undertaking; repayable on winding-up or after a long period, so (i).\n(D) Secured debentures: a fixed or floating charge on assets, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-012","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":12,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"The six common combinations of terms of issue and redemption of debentures are listed below:\n(i) Issued at par, redeemable at par\n(ii) Issued at discount, redeemable at par\n(iii) Issued at premium, redeemable at par\n(iv) Issued at par, redeemable at premium\n(v) Issued at discount, redeemable at premium\n(vi) Issued at premium, redeemable at premium\nIn how many of these cases is Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c debited at the time of allotment?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited whenever debentures are redeemable at a premium. That premium is a future liability accepted at the time of issue.\nCases (iv), (v) and (vi) are redeemable at a premium, so Loss on Issue A/c is debited in each of them.\nIn case (ii), the discount is debited to Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c, not Loss on Issue.\nCases (i) and (iii) involve no loss.\nTotal = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-013","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":13,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun Ltd. purchased its own debentures of Rs. 100 each, of total face value Rs. 50,000, from the open market at Rs. 96 each for immediate cancellation. What amount will finally be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,000","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 48,000","Rs. 50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: face value Rs. 50,000 ÷ Rs. 100 = 500 debentures, bought at Rs. 96 each.\nFormula: Profit on redemption = Face value − Price paid.\nSubstituting: Price paid = 500 × 96 = Rs. 48,000. Profit = 50,000 − 48,000 = Rs. 2,000.\nThe entries are Debentures A/c Dr. 50,000, To Bank 48,000, To Profit on Redemption 2,000. The profit is then transferred to Capital Reserve.\nSo the transfer to Capital Reserve = Rs. 2,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-014","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":14,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a company buys its own debentures in the open market below face value for immediate cancellation, the profit on redemption is transferred to Capital Reserve.\nReason (R): Profit on cancelling own debentures is a capital profit. It does not arise from the normal trading operations of the company.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. On purchase below par, Debentures A/c is debited and Bank and Profit on Redemption of Debentures A/c are credited. The profit is then transferred: Profit on Redemption A/c Dr. To Capital Reserve.\nR is true. The gain comes from settling a long-term liability for less than its face value, so it is a capital gain and not a trading profit.\nBecause it is a capital profit, it goes to Capital Reserve. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-015","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":15,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company took a bank loan and issued its own debentures to the bank as collateral security. It recorded the issue through a journal entry (Debenture Suspense A/c Dr. To Debentures A/c).\nAssertion (A): Debenture Suspense A/c appears as a deduction from debentures in the notes to Long-term Borrowings.\nReason (R): Debenture Suspense A/c is shown under the head Current Assets in the balance sheet.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Under the second method, the notes to Long-term Borrowings show the debentures with Debenture Suspense deducted from them. This leaves only the bank loan as the real liability.\nR is false. Debenture Suspense is not an asset at all. It merely cancels out the debentures, because no actual liability arises from a collateral issue.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-016","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":16,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Sona Ltd. issued 5,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 5%. What is the gross annual interest on these debentures?","image":"","options":["Rs. 50,000","Rs. 47,500","Rs. 52,500","Rs. 25,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 5,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each, coupon rate 10%, issued at a 5% discount.\nFormula: Interest = Nominal value × Rate. The discount is ignored.\nSubstituting: Nominal value = 5,000 × 100 = Rs. 5,00,000, so interest = 5,00,000 × 10% = Rs. 50,000.\nRs. 47,500 is wrong because it wrongly uses the issue price of Rs. 95.\nSo the annual interest = Rs. 50,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-017","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":17,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Type of debenture) with List-II (Feature):\nList-I\n(A) Bearer debentures\n(B) Registered debentures\n(C) Zero coupon rate debentures\n(D) Irredeemable debentures\nList-II\n(i) Issued at a substantial discount; the difference between nominal value and issue price is treated as interest\n(ii) Transferable by mere delivery\n(iii) Repayable on winding up or on the expiry of a long period\n(iv) Transferable only by executing a regular transfer deed","image":"","options":["(A)-(ii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(i), (D)-(iii)","(A)-(iv), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iii)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(i)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(i), (D)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Bearer debentures pass by delivery, and the company keeps no record of holders: (ii).\n(B) For registered debentures, holders' details are entered in a register, so transfer needs a transfer deed: (iv).\n(C) Zero coupon debentures carry no fixed interest rate. They are issued at a deep discount, and that discount compensates the investor: (i).\n(D) Irredeemable debentures are repaid on winding up or after a long period: (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-018","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":18,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company issues specific coupon rate debentures carrying a floating rate of interest. This floating rate is usually tagged with:","image":"","options":["The dividend rate on equity shares","The rate of securities premium","The bank rate","The rate of tax deducted at source"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Specific coupon rate debentures carry a stated rate of interest, called the coupon rate.\nThis rate may be fixed or floating.\nNCERT states that a floating rate is usually tagged with the bank rate.\nThe dividend rate varies with profits and has no link to debenture interest.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-019","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":19,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Saraswati Ltd. issued 50,000, 8% debentures of Rs. 40 each at a discount of Rs. 2 per debenture, redeemable at par. Its Securities Premium Reserve had a balance of Rs. 1,60,000. After the discount is written off as per NCERT, what balance will remain in the Securities Premium Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 60,000","Rs. 1,60,000","Nil","Rs. 80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 50,000 debentures, discount Rs. 2 each, Securities Premium Reserve (SPR) Rs. 1,60,000.\nFormula: Discount = number of debentures × discount per debenture.\nSubstituting: Discount = 50,000 × 2 = Rs. 1,00,000.\nSPR is large enough, so the full Rs. 1,00,000 is debited to SPR and nothing goes to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nSo the SPR balance left = 1,60,000 − 1,00,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-020","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":20,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company issues secured debentures with a fixed charge. Which of the following assets is most suitable for such a charge?","image":"","options":["Stock of finished goods meant for sale","Trade receivables collected during the year","Factory building used in operations","All general assets of the company taken together"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A fixed charge is created on a specific asset.\nSuch an asset is held for use in operations and is not meant for sale. A factory building fits this description.\nStock and receivables keep changing during business, so they are not suited to a fixed charge.\nA charge on all general assets taken together is a floating charge, not a fixed charge.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-021","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":21,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun Ltd. issued 8,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a 5% discount. The amount is payable Rs. 40 on application and Rs. 55 on allotment, and all money was duly received. The company had a balance of Rs. 25,000 in Securities Premium Reserve. The discount is written off in the year of issue. What amount will be debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss?","image":"","options":["Rs. 40,000","Rs. 25,000","Rs. 15,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 8,000 debentures at 5% discount on Rs. 100 face value.\nTotal discount = 8,000 × 5 = Rs. 40,000.\nThe discount is first written off from Securities Premium Reserve, to the extent of its balance: Rs. 25,000.\nThe remainder is charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss: 40,000 − 25,000 = Rs. 15,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-022","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":22,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. issued 5,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 6%. What amount will be debited to Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,70,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 30,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: 5,000 debentures, face value Rs. 100 each, discount 6%.\nFormula: Discount = Number of debentures × Face value × Rate of discount.\nSubstituting: 5,000 × 100 × 6% = 5,000 × 6 = Rs. 30,000.\nSo Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited with Rs. 30,000.\nRs. 4,70,000 is the cash actually received, not the discount.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-023","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":23,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"On April 1, Neel Ltd. issued 3,000, 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 10%. Interest is paid half-yearly on September 30 and March 31, and TDS is deducted @ 10%. What amount will be transferred from Debenture Interest A/c to the Statement of Profit and Loss for the year ending March 31?","image":"","options":["Rs. 36,000","Rs. 32,400","Rs. 39,600","Rs. 18,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Nominal value = 3,000 × 100 = Rs. 3,00,000; rate 12% p.a.; two half-yearly instalments.\nFormula: Each half-year's interest = 3,00,000 × 12% × 1/2 = Rs. 18,000.\nThe Debenture Interest A/c is debited with the gross amount each time, so its total = 18,000 × 2 = Rs. 36,000.\nTDS only splits the credit between Debentureholders and Income Tax Payable, so Rs. 32,400 is wrong.\nThe premium does not affect interest, so Rs. 39,600 is wrong.\nSo the amount transferred = Rs. 36,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-024","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":24,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Meera holds 12% secured non-convertible debentures, and Rohan holds equity shares of the same company. The company suffers a loss this year, and the board recommends no dividend. Which of the following statements correctly describes their positions?","image":"","options":["Rohan is entitled to dividend at a fixed rate, while the company may postpone Meera's interest because of the loss","Meera is entitled to 12% interest despite the loss, has a charge on the company's assets, but normally cannot vote; Rohan may receive no dividend but can vote at general meetings","Since Meera's debentures are secured, she becomes entitled to voting rights in a loss year","Both Meera and Rohan will get their capital back at the end of a specified period"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: dividend is an appropriation of profit and is not fixed. Interest is a charge and cannot be skipped.\n(2) Correct: interest at the fixed rate is payable even in a loss year. Secured debentures carry a charge on assets, and debentureholders normally have no vote. Shareholders may get no dividend but keep their voting rights.\n(3) Incorrect: security does not confer voting rights.\n(4) Incorrect: share money is normally not returned during the life of the company.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-025","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":25,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"On April 1, 2020, Sunrise Ltd. issued 4,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at par. Interest is paid annually on March 31. The debentures are redeemable at par in four equal annual instalments by draw of lots, at the end of the second, third, fourth and fifth years. What is the debenture interest for the fourth year (ending March 31, 2024)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 40,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Total debentures = 4,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 4,00,000. Each instalment = Rs. 1,00,000.\nStep 1: Instalments are redeemed at the end of year 2 and at the end of year 3.\nStep 2: Debentures outstanding during year 4 = 4,00,000 − 2,00,000 = Rs. 2,00,000.\nFormula: Interest = 10% of the outstanding face value.\nSubstituting: 10% × 2,00,000 = Rs. 20,000.\nRs. 40,000 is a wrong choice because it ignores the reducing balance.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-026","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":26,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Sona Ltd. purchased 400 of its own 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each from the open market at Rs. 98.50 each, for immediate cancellation. It also incurred brokerage and other expenses of Rs. 200 on the purchase. What amount will be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 600","Rs. 400","Rs. 800","Rs. 200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: face value = 400 × 100 = Rs. 40,000; purchase price = 400 × 98.50 = Rs. 39,400; expenses = Rs. 200.\nThe expenses form part of the cost of purchase: 39,400 + 200 = Rs. 39,600.\nProfit = 40,000 − 39,600 = Rs. 400.\nEntries: Profit on Redemption A/c is credited Rs. 400, then transferred to Capital Reserve.\nRs. 600 ignores the expenses.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-027","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":27,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"In an over-subscribed issue of debentures, some applicants are given a pro-rata allotment. Their surplus application money is retained by the company. In the journal entry, this surplus is credited to:","image":"","options":["Debentures A/c","Bank A/c","Debenture Allotment A/c","Debenture Suspense A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: the surplus application money of pro-rata applicants is adjusted against the allotment money due from them. So it is credited to Debenture Allotment A/c.\nDebentures A/c is credited only with the application money on debentures actually allotted.\nBank A/c is credited only for refunds.\nDebenture Suspense A/c is used for collateral security, not for this.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-028","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":28,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Most debentures issued by companies are non-convertible debentures.\n(ii) Redeemable debentures can be redeemed only at par.\n(iii) Interest on bearer debentures is paid to the person who produces the interest coupon.\n(iv) Registered debentures can be transferred merely by delivery.\n(v) Debentures may be converted into equity shares at the option of either the company or the debentureholders, depending on the terms.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT says most debentures fall in the non-convertible category.\n(ii) Incorrect: redeemable debentures can be redeemed at par or at a premium.\n(iii) Correct: interest goes to whoever presents the coupon.\n(iv) Incorrect: registered debentures need a regular transfer deed.\n(v) Correct: conversion may be at the option of the company or of the holders.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-029","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":29,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company had recorded debentures issued as collateral security using the Debenture Suspense account. When it repays the bank loan in full, the entry passed is:","image":"","options":["Bank Loan A/c Dr.\n To Debentures A/c","Debentures A/c Dr.\n To Debenture Suspense A/c","Debentures A/c Dr.\n To Bank A/c","Debenture Suspense A/c Dr.\n To Bank A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"On repayment of the loan, the collateral debentures are no longer needed.\nThey are cancelled by reversing the original entry: Debentures A/c Dr. To Debenture Suspense A/c.\nNo cash is paid to anyone for these debentures, so options (3) and (4) are wrong.\nOption (1) does not reverse the suspense entry at all.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-030","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":30,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Mehta Ltd. took over the business of Arora & Sons. It acquired assets of ₹5,00,000 and took over liabilities of ₹60,000, for a purchase consideration of ₹4,80,000 payable in 10% debentures at par. What amount will be debited to Goodwill A/c in the books of Mehta Ltd.?","image":"","options":["₹20,000","₹60,000","₹40,000","₹1,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: assets = ₹5,00,000; liabilities = ₹60,000; purchase consideration = ₹4,80,000.\nNet assets = 5,00,000 − 60,000 = ₹4,40,000.\nThe consideration exceeds net assets, so the excess is goodwill.\nGoodwill = 4,80,000 − 4,40,000 = ₹40,000.\nOption (1) ignores the liabilities taken over (5,00,000 − 4,80,000 = 20,000), which gives the wrong amount.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-031","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":31,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Debentures were originally issued at par and are redeemable at a premium of 10%. They are redeemed in a lump sum on maturity. In the entry that makes the amount due to debentureholders, which account is debited along with the Debentures A/c?","image":"","options":["Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c","Securities Premium Reserve A/c","Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c","Debenture Redemption Investment A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The premium payable on redemption was credited to Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c at the time of issue. It is a liability until the debentures are paid off.\nOn redemption the entry is: Debentures A/c Dr., Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c Dr., To Debentureholders.\nLoss on Issue of Debentures A/c is wrong because it was debited at issue and already written off.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-032","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":32,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Under Schedule III of the Companies Act, 2013, how are 12% debentures redeemable after 8 years shown in the balance sheet of the issuing company?","image":"","options":["Shareholders' Funds → Share Capital","Current Liabilities → Short-term borrowings","Non-current Liabilities → Long-term borrowings","Non-current Liabilities → Long-term provisions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A debenture acknowledges a debt, so it is part of borrowed capital. It is not owned capital, which rules out Share Capital.\nThese debentures are repayable after more than 12 months. So they are shown under Non-current Liabilities, sub-head Long-term borrowings.\nThey are not short-term borrowings, because repayment is not due within 12 months.\nThey are not provisions either.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-033","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":33,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Arya Ltd., a listed company, has 2,500, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each maturing for redemption on January 31, 2026. As per the Companies Act, what is the minimum amount it must invest or deposit on or before April 30, 2025 as Debenture Redemption Investment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 25,000","Rs. 22,500","Rs. 2,50,000","Rs. 37,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: debentures maturing = 2,500 × 100 = Rs. 2,50,000.\nRule: a sum not less than 15% of the debentures maturing during the year must be invested by April 30. This applies to listed companies as well.\nSubstituting: 15% × 2,50,000 = Rs. 37,500.\nRs. 25,000 (10%) is the DRR rate for 'other unlisted' companies, not the DRI rate.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-034","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":34,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about interest on debentures are correct?\n(i) It must be paid whether or not the company earns a profit.\n(ii) Tax is deducted at source from it as per the Income Tax Act, 1961, if it exceeds the prescribed limit.\n(iii) Income Tax Payable A/c is an asset of the company until the TDS is deposited.\n(iv) Debentureholders can adjust the tax deducted at source against the tax due from them.\n(v) It is calculated on the issue price of the debentures.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: interest is a charge against profit.\n(ii) Correct: TDS applies under the Income Tax Act, 1961, when interest exceeds the prescribed limit.\n(iii) Incorrect: TDS is owed to the government, so it is a liability until it is deposited.\n(iv) Correct: debentureholders can adjust TDS against their own tax due.\n(v) Incorrect: interest is calculated on the nominal value.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-035","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":35,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"On April 1, 2019, Neel Ltd. had Rs. 50,000 in Securities Premium Reserve. On June 1, 2019 it issued 8,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a 10% discount, redeemable at a 10% premium. On December 1, 2019 it issued 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 2 per share. The loss on issue of debentures is written off on March 31, 2020. What amount will be charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss?","image":"","options":["Rs. 10,000","Rs. 1,10,000","Nil","Rs. 30,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1 – Loss on issue: discount 10% + premium on redemption 10% = 20% of Rs. 8,00,000 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nStep 2 – SPR available on March 31: opening Rs. 50,000 + share premium (50,000 × 2) Rs. 1,00,000 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nStep 3 – Charge to the Statement of Profit and Loss: 1,60,000 − 1,50,000 = Rs. 10,000.\nWhy Rs. 1,10,000 is wrong: it ignores the share premium received during the year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-036","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":36,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Rudra Ltd. is an 'other unlisted' company. It has outstanding 8,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each. Of these, 2,000 debentures are due for redemption during the year ending March 31 of the next year.\nStatement I: The adequate Debenture Redemption Reserve for Rudra Ltd. is Rs. 80,000.\nStatement II: Rudra Ltd. must invest or deposit at least Rs. 30,000 in specified securities or deposits on or before April 30.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: for 'other unlisted' companies, adequate DRR = 10% of outstanding debentures = 10% × 8,00,000 = Rs. 80,000. Correct.\nStatement II: the investment must be at least 15% of debentures maturing during the next year = 15% × (2,000 × 100) = 15% × 2,00,000 = Rs. 30,000, made on or before April 30. Correct.\nNote that DRR is based on all outstanding debentures, while DRI is based only on the debentures maturing next year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-037","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":37,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Riya Ltd. purchased some of its own debentures in the open market at a price below their face value and cancelled them immediately. The profit on redemption is finally transferred to:","image":"","options":["Statement of Profit and Loss","Capital Reserve","General Reserve","Securities Premium Reserve"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Buying own debentures below face value for cancellation is a profit arising from a capital transaction.\nThe company first credits it to Profit on Redemption of Debentures A/c.\nIt then passes the entry: Profit on Redemption of Debentures A/c Dr. To Capital Reserve.\nThe Statement of Profit and Loss is the tempting option, but it is used only when there is a loss, i.e., when debentures are bought above par.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-038","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":38,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Debentures can be converted into equity shares if the terms of issue so provide.\nStatement II: Shares can similarly be converted into debentures at the option of the shareholders.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Debentures can be converted into shares when the terms of issue allow it; such debentures are called convertible debentures.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT clearly states that shares cannot be converted into debentures.\nConvertibility therefore works in one direction only, from debentures to shares.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-039","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":39,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The part of discount on issue of debentures that is to be written off within 12 months of the balance sheet date is shown under 'Other Current Assets'.\nReason (R): The Companies Act, 2013 does not allow a company to issue debentures at a discount.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Discount to be written off within 12 months (or within the operating cycle) is shown under Other Current Assets. The portion to be written off after 12 months goes under Other Non-current Assets.\nR is false. The Companies Act, 2013 imposes no restriction on issuing debentures at a discount.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-040","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":40,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company buys its own debentures in the open market at a price below their face value and cancels them immediately. The profit on this redemption is finally transferred to which account?","image":"","options":["Statement of Profit and Loss","Capital Reserve","Securities Premium Reserve","Debenture Redemption Reserve"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: When debentures are bought below face value and cancelled, the company debits the Debentures A/c with the face value. It credits Bank with the price paid and credits the difference to Profit on Redemption of Debentures A/c.\nStep 2: This profit comes from a capital transaction, not from trading. So it is transferred to Capital Reserve.\nStep 3: Statement of Profit and Loss is a tempting choice, but it is used for the opposite case. When debentures are bought above face value, the loss is written off there.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-041","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":41,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the terms of issue and redemption of debentures is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Premium on redemption of debentures is a liability of the company payable in future.","Discount or loss on issue of debentures is a capital loss.","Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c is shown under 'Securities Premium' in Reserves and Surplus.","When debentures are issued at a premium and redeemable at a premium, Loss on Issue A/c is debited only with the premium on redemption."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the premium on redemption is payable to debentureholders at maturity, so it is a future liability.\n(2) Correct: NCERT treats discount or loss on issue as a capital loss.\n(3) Incorrect: Premium on Redemption is shown under Long-term Borrowings, not under Securities Premium. Securities Premium Reserve holds only premium received on issue.\n(4) Correct: the premium received on issue goes to Securities Premium Reserve, so the loss equals only the redemption premium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-042","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":42,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company issues debentures and asks for the whole amount in one instalment on application. On receipt of the application money, which account is credited?","image":"","options":["Debentures A/c","Debenture Application & Allotment A/c","Debenture Allotment A/c","Securities Premium Reserve A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When the full amount is payable in one instalment, the money received is first credited to a combined Debenture Application & Allotment A/c. The entry is: Bank A/c Dr. To Debenture Application & Allotment A/c.\nOn allotment, this account is closed by transferring it to Debentures A/c.\nDebentures A/c is tempting, but it is credited only at the allotment stage, not when cash is received.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-043","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":43,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"In the context of a bank loan, debentures issued as collateral security are best described as:","image":"","options":["The principal security against which the loan is first realised","A subsidiary or additional security given besides the primary security","Debentures sold to the public to raise money to repay the loan","Debentures issued to vendors in exchange for assets"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines collateral security as a subsidiary, secondary or additional security given besides the primary security when a company obtains a loan or overdraft.\nThe lender uses it only if the sale of the primary security does not cover the loan in full.\nOption (1) describes the primary security, not the collateral.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe issues for cash and for consideration other than cash.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-044","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":44,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. took over assets of Rs. 6,00,000 and liabilities of Rs. 1,00,000 from Vardhan & Co. The agreed purchase consideration was Rs. 5,61,600. It was discharged by issuing 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 8%. How many debentures will Neel Ltd. issue?","image":"","options":["5,616","5,200","5,000","6,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: purchase consideration Rs. 5,61,600; issue price = 100 + 8 = Rs. 108.\nFormula: Number of debentures = Purchase consideration ÷ Issue price.\nSubstituting: 5,61,600 ÷ 108 = 5,200.\nNet assets are 6,00,000 − 1,00,000 = Rs. 5,00,000, so the excess of Rs. 61,600 is debited to Goodwill.\nOption 5,616 wrongly divides by the face value instead of the issue price.\nSo the number of debentures = 5,200.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-045","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":45,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Profit made on purchasing own debentures from the open market below face value for cancellation is transferred to Capital Reserve.\nReason (R): Profit on redemption of debentures is a revenue profit available for distribution as dividend.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The entry is: Profit on Redemption of Debentures A/c Dr. To Capital Reserve.\nR is false. This profit arises from a capital transaction, not from normal operations, so it is a capital profit. That is exactly why it goes to Capital Reserve and not to the Statement of Profit and Loss for dividend.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-046","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":46,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (item) with List-II (where it appears in the balance sheet):\nList-I\n(A) 12% Debentures issued\n(B) Premium received on issue of debentures\n(C) Discount on issue to be written off within 12 months\n(D) Discount on issue to be written off after 12 months\nList-II\n(i) Other Non-current Assets\n(ii) Reserves and Surplus\n(iii) Long-term borrowings\n(iv) Other Current Assets","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Debentures are borrowed capital and are shown under Non-current liabilities, Long-term borrowings: (iii).\n(B) Premium on issue is credited to Securities Premium Reserve, shown under Reserves and Surplus: (ii).\n(C) Discount to be written off within 12 months is shown under Other Current Assets: (iv).\n(D) Discount to be written off after 12 months is shown under Other Non-current Assets: (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-047","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":47,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When debentures of Rs. 100 each are issued at Rs. 110, the Debentures A/c is credited with only Rs. 100 per debenture.\nReason (R): The excess of issue price over the face value is credited to Securities Premium Reserve A/c.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The Debentures A/c always records the nominal (face) value, because that is the debt the company owes.\nR is true. The premium of Rs. 10 is credited separately to Securities Premium Reserve A/c.\nR explains A. Since the premium goes to a separate account, only the face value remains in the Debentures A/c.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-048","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":48,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"On April 1, 2021, Neel Ltd. issued 4,000, 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each. Interest is payable annually on March 31. The debentures are redeemable at par in four equal annual instalments, starting at the end of the 2nd year. What is the debenture interest for the 3rd year (year ending March 31, 2024)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 48,000","Rs. 36,000","Rs. 24,000","Rs. 12,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 4,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each, with 1,000 redeemed at the end of each year from Year 2.\nInterest is charged on the outstanding (reducing) nominal value.\nAt the end of Year 2, 1,000 debentures are redeemed, so 3,000 are outstanding during Year 3.\nSubstituting: 3,000 × 100 × 12% = Rs. 36,000.\nRs. 48,000 is the tempting wrong option because it ignores the redemption already made.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-049","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":49,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When an 'other unlisted' company has redeemed all its debentures, the balance in its Debenture Redemption Reserve is transferred to General Reserve.\nStatement II: Companies listed on a stock exchange are exempted from creating a Debenture Redemption Reserve.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. After redemption, DRR is no longer required, so the entry is: DRR A/c Dr. To General Reserve. This is shown in Illustration 22(b).\nStatement II is correct. Under the Act, listed companies are among those exempted from creating DRR. They only make the 15% Debenture Redemption Investment.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-050","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":50,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun Ltd. issued 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at Rs. 108 each. Where will the extra Rs. 8 per debenture be credited, and where will it appear in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Debentures A/c; under Long-term borrowings","Securities Premium Reserve A/c; under Reserves and Surplus","Capital Reserve A/c; under Long-term provisions","Statement of Profit and Loss; under Other Income"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The Rs. 8 above the face value is premium on issue.\nPremium is credited to Securities Premium Reserve A/c. It is shown under Shareholders' Funds, in the sub-head Reserves and Surplus.\nThe Debentures A/c is credited only with the nominal value of Rs. 100. So option (1) is wrong.\nThe premium is a capital receipt, not income. So it is never taken to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-051","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":51,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Mehta Ltd. redeems its debentures in instalments, with a fixed part of the liability repaid each year. How does the company decide which particular debentures are to be redeemed in a given year?","image":"","options":["By purchasing them at the lowest market price","By drawing the requisite number of lots out of the outstanding debentures","By redeeming debentures of holders who apply first","By redeeming debentures with the highest coupon rate first"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under redemption in instalments, the total debenture liability is spread over the specified years.\nNCERT states that the actual debentures to be redeemed each time are identified by drawing the required number of lots.\nOption (1) describes purchase in the open market, which is a separate method. Options (3) and (4) are not the NCERT procedure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-052","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":52,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Sunrise Ltd. purchased the business of Moon Traders. It took over building of Rs. 3,50,000 and machinery of Rs. 2,50,000, and assumed creditors of Rs. 1,00,000. The purchase consideration of Rs. 4,80,000 was settled by issuing 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 4%.\nWhich of the following correctly describes the entries in the books of Sunrise Ltd.?","image":"","options":["Capital Reserve Rs. 20,000; 5,000 debentures issued; 9% Debentures A/c credited with Rs. 5,00,000","Goodwill Rs. 20,000; 4,800 debentures issued; 9% Debentures A/c credited with Rs. 4,80,000","Capital Reserve Rs. 20,000; 4,800 debentures issued; Discount on Issue of Debentures Rs. 19,200","Goodwill Rs. 20,000; 5,000 debentures issued; Discount on Issue of Debentures Rs. 20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Net assets = (3,50,000 + 2,50,000) − 1,00,000 = Rs. 5,00,000.\nStep 2: Consideration (Rs. 4,80,000) is less than net assets, so Capital Reserve = Rs. 20,000. Goodwill does not arise, which rules out options (2) and (4).\nStep 3: Issue price = 100 − 4 = Rs. 96, so number of debentures = 4,80,000 ÷ 96 = 5,000.\nStep 4: Debentures A/c is credited at face value: 5,000 × 100 = Rs. 5,00,000. Discount on Issue of Debentures = Rs. 20,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-053","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":53,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the over-subscription of debentures are correct?\n(a) A company may allot all the debentures applied for, if it wishes, even beyond the number offered.\n(b) Money refunded to rejected applicants is recorded by crediting Bank A/c.\n(c) Surplus application money of pro-rata applicants may be adjusted towards allotment and later calls.\n(d) Debenture Allotment A/c is debited with the allotment money on all the debentures applied for.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Incorrect: a company cannot allot more debentures than it has invited.\n(b) Correct: a refund means cash goes out, so Bank A/c is credited.\n(c) Correct: NCERT permits retaining surplus money for allotment and calls.\n(d) Incorrect: allotment money is due only on the debentures actually allotted (the number offered), not on all applied for.\nSo 2 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-054","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":54,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. issued 8,000, 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 5%. The amount is payable Rs. 30 on application and Rs. 65 on allotment. The issue was fully subscribed. What amount will be credited to 12% Debentures A/c in the allotment entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,20,000","Rs. 5,60,000","Rs. 6,00,000","Rs. 4,80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: face value Rs. 100 and discount Rs. 5. Application money is Rs. 30 and allotment money is Rs. 65.\nOn allotment, the Debentures A/c receives the rest of the face value: 100 − 30 = Rs. 70 per debenture.\nThis Rs. 70 is made up of the Rs. 65 allotment money due plus the Rs. 5 discount.\nSubstituting: 8,000 × 70 = Rs. 5,60,000.\nEntry: Debenture Allotment A/c Dr. 5,20,000 and Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c Dr. 40,000, To 12% Debentures A/c 5,60,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-055","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":55,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"On July 1, 2023, Ravi Ltd. issued 6,000, 8% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 5%, redeemable at a premium of 5%. Interest is payable on September 30 and March 31 each year, and TDS is deducted @ 10%. The books close on March 31. What amount of debenture interest will be debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss for 2023-24?","image":"","options":["Rs. 36,000","Rs. 48,000","Rs. 34,200","Rs. 32,400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Nominal value = 6,000 × 100 = Rs. 6,00,000; rate 8% p.a.; debentures outstanding from July 1, 2023 to March 31, 2024, which is 9 months.\nFormula: Interest = Nominal value × Rate × Period.\nSubstituting: 6,00,000 × 8% × 9/12 = Rs. 36,000. This is Rs. 12,000 for Jul–Sep plus Rs. 24,000 for Oct–Mar.\nThe discount and redemption premium do not change interest, and gross interest (not net of TDS) is charged to P&L.\nRs. 32,400 is the net-of-TDS figure, so it is wrong.\nSo the interest debited = Rs. 36,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-056","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":56,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Delta Ltd., an 'other unlisted' company, has 6,000, 12% debentures of Rs. 50 each outstanding. They are redeemable in four equal annual instalments. What amount must Delta Ltd. transfer from Surplus (Balance in Statement of Profit and Loss) to Debenture Redemption Reserve to meet the adequacy requirement?","image":"","options":["Rs. 11,250","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 75,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 6,000 debentures × Rs. 50 = Rs. 3,00,000 outstanding.\nRule: for 'other unlisted' companies, DRR must be 10% of the value of outstanding debentures.\nCalculation: 10% × 3,00,000 = Rs. 30,000.\nRs. 45,000 wrongly applies 15%, which is the DRI rate.\nRs. 11,250 is the DRI on one instalment (15% of Rs. 75,000), not the DRR.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-057","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":57,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A company may redeem its debentures before the maturity date if its articles provide for such redemption.\nStatement II: A company may redeem its debentures only out of profits and never out of capital.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Debentures are generally redeemed on the due date, but NCERT notes that early redemption is allowed if the articles permit it.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that a company may source redemption either out of capital or out of profits. For example, exempt companies may redeem out of capital.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-058","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":58,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT according to NCERT?","image":"","options":["Discount or loss on issue of debentures is a capital loss and is written off in the year of issue.","Discount or loss on issue is written off first from Securities Premium Reserve; any balance is written off from the Statement of Profit and Loss.","Premium on redemption of debentures is shown under 'Reserves and Surplus' along with Securities Premium Reserve.","The Companies Act, 2013 does not impose any restriction on issue of debentures at a discount."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT treats discount or loss on issue as a capital loss, written off in the year of issue.\n(2) Correct: Securities Premium Reserve is used first, to the extent of its balance. The rest is charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\n(3) Incorrect: Premium on redemption is a future liability, that is, a provision. It is shown under Non-current Liabilities, sub-head Long-term Borrowings, until the debentures are redeemed.\n(4) Correct: The Act places no restriction on issuing debentures at a discount.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-059","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":59,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. offered 20,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each. Rs. 40 was payable on application and Rs. 60 on allotment. Applications were received for 26,000 debentures. Applications for 4,000 debentures were rejected. Applicants for 22,000 debentures were allotted 20,000 debentures pro-rata. All money due was received. What amount was actually received on allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 12,00,000","Rs. 11,20,000","Rs. 9,60,000","Rs. 10,40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: allotment due = 20,000 × 60 = Rs. 12,00,000.\nExcess debentures applied for by pro-rata applicants = 22,000 − 20,000 = 2,000.\nSurplus retained = 2,000 × 40 = Rs. 80,000. This is adjusted against allotment.\nCash received on allotment = 12,00,000 − 80,000 = Rs. 11,20,000\nRs. 9,60,000 is wrong because it also deducts the money of rejected applicants, which is refunded, not adjusted.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-060","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":60,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A holder of convertible debentures may, under the terms of issue, later become a shareholder of the company.\nStatement II: A floating charge on the general assets of the company also covers assets already assigned to secured creditors under a fixed charge.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Convertible debentures can be converted into equity shares if the terms provide. The holder then becomes an owner of the company.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that a floating charge involves all assets excluding those assigned to the secured creditors.\nSo assets under a fixed charge fall outside the floating charge.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-061","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":61,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Mr. Arjun holds debentures of a company. The company keeps no record of who the holders are. He can transfer them to his friend simply by handing them over. These debentures are:","image":"","options":["Registered debentures","Bearer debentures","Secured debentures","Convertible debentures"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Bearer debentures are transferred by mere delivery.\nThe company keeps no record of their holders.\nInterest is paid to whoever produces the interest coupon attached to the debenture.\nRegistered debentures are the opposite case: holders' details are kept in a register, and transfer needs a regular transfer deed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-062","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":62,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Read the statements about redemption of debentures.\nStatement I: A company may redeem its debentures before the maturity date if its articles provide for such redemption.\nStatement II: When debentures are redeemed in instalments, the particular debentures to be redeemed each time are usually selected by draw of lots.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Debentures are generally redeemed on the due date, but NCERT allows redemption before maturity if the articles provide for it.\nStatement II is correct. Under the instalment method, the total liability is spread over the years. The actual debentures redeemed each year are identified by drawing lots.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-063","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":63,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (accounting effect):\nList-I\n(A) Debentures issued to vendor at par\n(B) Debentures issued to vendor at a premium\n(C) Debentures issued to vendor at a discount\n(D) Net assets taken over exceed purchase consideration\nList-II\n(i) Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited\n(ii) Capital Reserve is credited\n(iii) Only Vendor is debited and Debentures A/c is credited\n(iv) Securities Premium Reserve A/c is credited","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) At par: Vendor Dr. To Debentures A/c, with no other account involved, so (iii).\n(B) At a premium: the excess over face value is credited to Securities Premium Reserve, so (iv).\n(C) At a discount: Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited along with the vendor, so (i).\n(D) A purchase below the value of net assets gives a capital gain, credited to Capital Reserve, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-064","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":64,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are exempted from creating a Debenture Redemption Reserve (DRR) and may redeem debentures out of capital?\n(i) Companies listed on a stock exchange\n(ii) Banking companies\n(iii) NBFCs registered with the Reserve Bank of India\n(iv) Housing finance companies registered with the National Housing Bank\n(v) 'Other unlisted' companies","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Exempted: listed companies need not create DRR.\n(ii) Exempted: banking companies.\n(iii) Exempted: NBFCs registered with the RBI.\n(iv) Exempted: housing finance companies registered with the NHB.\n(v) Not exempted: 'other unlisted' companies must keep DRR equal to 10% of outstanding debentures.\nSo 4 of the 5 are exempted.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-065","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":65,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Irredeemable debentures are also called perpetual debentures.\nReason (R): The company gives no undertaking to repay the money raised through such debentures during its lifetime. They are repayable only on winding up or after a very long period.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that irredeemable debentures are also known as perpetual debentures.\nR is true. The company gives no undertaking to repay them. They are repaid on winding up or on the expiry of a long period.\nR is the correct explanation of A, because the absence of any repayment undertaking is exactly why such debentures are called perpetual.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-066","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":66,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Irredeemable debentures are also called perpetual debentures. The company gives no undertaking to repay them except on winding up or after a long period.\nStatement II: Bearer debentures can be transferred only by executing a regular transfer deed. The company keeps a register of their holders.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT says irredeemable (perpetual) debentures carry no undertaking for repayment. They are repaid on winding up or after a long period.\nStatement II is incorrect. It describes registered debentures. Bearer debentures are transferred by mere delivery. The company keeps no record of their holders, and interest is paid to whoever presents the coupon.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-067","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":67,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nStatement I: Payment of interest on debentures is an appropriation of profits, so the company pays it only when it earns a profit.\nStatement II: Dividend on shares may vary from year to year depending on the profits of the company.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true and Statement II is false","Statement I is false and Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. Interest on debentures is a charge on profits, not an appropriation of profits. It must be paid even if there is no profit.\nStatement II is true. The rate of dividend depends on profits and can change every year. The rate of interest on debentures, by contrast, is fixed in advance.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-068","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":68,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When debentures redeemable at a premium are allotted, the premium payable on redemption is credited to Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c at the time of issue itself.\nReason (R): Premium on redemption is a liability of the company that is payable in future, when the debentures are redeemed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In the allotment entry for debentures redeemable at a premium, Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c is credited at once. Loss on Issue A/c is debited with the same amount.\nR is true. NCERT describes this premium as a liability payable in future, shown under Long-term Borrowings until redemption.\nThe company records it on the issue date because it is committed to paying it. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-069","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":69,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Arya Ltd. issued 2,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 5%. The debentures are redeemable at a premium of 10%. What amount will be debited to 'Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c' at the time of allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 10,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 2,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each; discount 5%; premium on redemption 10%.\nRule: When debentures are issued at a discount and redeemable at a premium, Loss on Issue = discount on issue + premium on redemption.\nDiscount = 2,000 × 5 = Rs. 10,000.\nPremium = 2,000 × 10 = Rs. 20,000.\nSo the loss on issue = 10,000 + 20,000 = Rs. 30,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-070","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":70,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are permitted avenues for the Debenture Redemption Investment of 15%?\n(i) Deposit with a scheduled bank, free from any charge or lien\n(ii) Securities of the Central Government\n(iii) Purchase of the company's own equity shares\n(iv) Securities of a State Government\n(v) Bonds of a company notified under sub-clause (f) of section 20 of the Indian Trusts Act","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Permitted: a deposit with a scheduled bank free from charge or lien.\n(ii) Permitted: Central Government securities.\n(iii) Not permitted: buying the company's own shares is not an approved avenue.\n(iv) Permitted: State Government securities.\n(v) Permitted: bonds of a company notified under section 20(f) of the Indian Trusts Act.\nSo 4 avenues are permitted.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-071","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":71,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Rohan Ltd. issued 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at Rs. 108 each. Where will the Rs. 8 per debenture appear in the company's balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Non-current liabilities: Long-term borrowings","Other Non-current Assets","Shareholders' Funds: Reserves and Surplus, as Securities Premium Reserve","Current liabilities: Other current liabilities"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The issue price (Rs. 108) is more than the face value (Rs. 100), so Rs. 8 per debenture is premium on issue.\nThe premium is credited to Securities Premium Reserve A/c.\nIt is shown under Shareholders' Funds, in the sub-head Reserves and Surplus.\nOnly the face value of the debentures goes to Long-term borrowings. The premium is not borrowed money.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-072","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":72,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Aravali Ltd. issued 300, 9% debentures of Rs. 500 each at a discount of 8%, redeemable at a premium of 6% after 5 years. On the date of issue, the company had a balance of Rs. 15,000 in Securities Premium Reserve. It wrote off the loss on issue in the same year, using Securities Premium Reserve first.\nWhat amount will be charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss?","image":"","options":["Rs. 6,000","Rs. 9,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 21,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Nominal value = 300 × Rs. 500 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nDiscount = 8% = Rs. 12,000. Redemption premium = 6% = Rs. 9,000.\nLoss on Issue = Rs. 12,000 + Rs. 9,000 = Rs. 21,000.\nWrite-off: Securities Premium Reserve is used to the extent of its balance, Rs. 15,000. The rest is charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nSo the charge to the Statement of Profit and Loss = Rs. 21,000 − Rs. 15,000 = Rs. 6,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-073","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":73,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the methods of redeeming debentures described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Payment in lump sum at maturity","Payment in instalments during the tenure","Purchase of own debentures in the open market for cancellation","Issue of bonus shares to debentureholders out of free reserves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four methods of redemption: (i) payment in lump sum, (ii) payment in instalments, (iii) purchase in the open market and (iv) conversion into shares or new debentures.\nOptions (1), (2) and (3) are all on this list.\nBonus shares are issued to shareholders, not debentureholders. Issuing them does not discharge the debenture liability.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-074","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":74,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. issued 2,000, 9% debentures of Rs 100 each at a discount of 5%. They are redeemable at a premium of 10% after 5 years. What amount will be debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c at the time of issue?","image":"","options":["Rs 10,000","Rs 20,000","Rs 30,000","Rs 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 2,000 debentures, face value Rs 100, discount 5%, premium on redemption 10%.\nRule: When debentures are issued at a discount and redeemable at a premium, Loss on Issue A/c is debited with the discount plus the premium on redemption.\nWorking: Discount = 2,000 × 5 = Rs 10,000. Premium on redemption = 2,000 × 10 = Rs 20,000.\nLoss on issue = 10,000 + 20,000 = Rs 30,000.\nRs 20,000 is the premium alone, a common error.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-075","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":75,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a company buys assets from a vendor, it debits Sundry Assets A/c and credits the Vendor's A/c with the purchase consideration.\nStatement II: When debentures are issued to the vendor in settlement, Bank A/c is debited because the vendor is treated as having paid for the debentures.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. On purchase, the assets come in and the vendor becomes a creditor, so the entry is Sundry Assets A/c Dr., To Vendor's A/c.\nStatement II is incorrect. No cash changes hands when debentures are issued for consideration other than cash.\nThe vendor's account is debited to discharge the liability: Vendor's A/c Dr., To Debentures A/c.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-076","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":76,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"On April 1, 2024, Neel Ltd. issued 5,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 5%. Interest is payable half-yearly on September 30 and March 31. Tax is deducted at source at 10%. What amount will be credited to Debentureholders A/c on September 30, 2024?","image":"","options":["Rs. 20,250","Rs. 22,500","Rs. 19,237.50","Rs. 40,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Nominal value = 5,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 5,00,000. Interest rate is 9% p.a., paid half-yearly. TDS is 10%.\nInterest is calculated on the nominal value, not on the issue price of Rs. 95.\nHalf-yearly interest = 5,00,000 × 9% × 6/12 = Rs. 22,500. This amount is debited to Debenture Interest A/c.\nTDS = 10% of 22,500 = Rs. 2,250. This is credited to Income Tax Payable A/c.\nNet amount credited to Debentureholders A/c = 22,500 − 2,250 = Rs. 20,250.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-077","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":77,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Premium payable on redemption of debentures is shown in the balance sheet under the head 'Reserves and Surplus' until the debentures are redeemed.\nStatement II: When a company buys its own debentures in the open market below face value for cancellation, the profit on redemption is transferred to Capital Reserve.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Premium on redemption is a liability payable in the future. NCERT shows it under Non-current Liabilities, sub-head 'Long-term Borrowings', not under Reserves and Surplus.\nStatement II is correct. The entry is: Debentures A/c Dr. To Bank A/c, To Profit on Redemption of Debentures A/c. This profit is then transferred to Capital Reserve.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-078","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":78,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A company must pay interest on its debentures even in a year in which it incurs a loss.\nReason (R): Interest on debentures is a charge against the profits of the company.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: interest is a contractual obligation and must be paid whether or not the company earns a profit.\nR is true: debenture interest is a charge against profit, not an appropriation of profit like dividend.\nR explains A. Because interest is a charge, it does not depend on profits being available.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-079","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":79,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (basis of classification of debentures) with List-II (type of debenture):\nList-I\n(A) Security\n(B) Tenure\n(C) Registration\n(D) Coupon rate\nList-II\n(i) Bearer debentures\n(ii) Deep discount debentures\n(iii) Naked debentures\n(iv) Perpetual debentures","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Security: unsecured debentures are also called naked debentures, so A-(iii).\n(B) Tenure: irredeemable debentures are also called perpetual debentures, so B-(iv).\n(C) Registration: bearer debentures are transferred by mere delivery, so C-(i).\n(D) Coupon rate: zero coupon debentures are also called deep discount debentures, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-080","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":80,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. issued 2,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 6%. The debentures are redeemable at a premium of 4% after 5 years. What amount will be debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c at the time of allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 8,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 4,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 2,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each; discount on issue 6%; premium on redemption 4%.\nRule: When debentures are issued at a discount and redeemable at a premium, Loss on Issue A/c is debited with discount plus premium.\nSubstituting: Discount = 2,000 × Rs. 6 = Rs. 12,000; Premium = 2,000 × Rs. 4 = Rs. 8,000.\nSo Loss on Issue of Debentures = Rs. 12,000 + Rs. 8,000 = Rs. 20,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-081","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":81,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. acquired assets of Rs. 8,40,000 and took over creditors of Rs. 1,20,000 from Raj Enterprises. As the full purchase consideration, it issued 7,500, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 4%. What amount will be debited to Goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 1,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: assets = Rs. 8,40,000; creditors = Rs. 1,20,000; 7,500 debentures issued at Rs. 104 each.\nStep 1: Purchase consideration = 7,500 × 104 = Rs. 7,80,000.\nStep 2: Net assets = 8,40,000 − 1,20,000 = Rs. 7,20,000.\nStep 3: Consideration exceeds net assets, so Goodwill = 7,80,000 − 7,20,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nOption (1) wrongly values the consideration at face value (Rs. 7,50,000).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-082","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":82,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Sarthak Ltd. issued 20,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 7%, redeemable at par. On that date its Securities Premium Reserve had a balance of Rs. 90,000. What amount of the discount will be written off to the Statement of Profit and Loss in the year of issue?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,40,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 50,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: discount = 20,000 × Rs. 7 = Rs. 1,40,000; Securities Premium Reserve = Rs. 90,000.\nRule: the discount is a capital loss, written off in the year of issue. It is first adjusted against Securities Premium Reserve to the extent of the balance. Any remainder goes to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nSubstituting: Amount to the Statement of Profit and Loss = 1,40,000 − 90,000 = Rs. 50,000.\nSo Rs. 50,000 is charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-083","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":83,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Delta Ltd. offered 8,000, 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each. Rs. 60 was payable on application and Rs. 40 on allotment. There are no further calls. Applications were received for 20,000 debentures. Applications for 2,000 debentures were rejected. Applicants for 18,000 debentures were allotted 8,000 debentures pro-rata. Surplus application money is adjusted against allotment, and any amount still left over is refunded. What is the total amount refunded by the company?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,00,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 7,20,000","Rs. 2,80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: total application money = 20,000 × 60 = Rs. 12,00,000.\nTransferred to Debentures A/c = 8,000 × 60 = Rs. 4,80,000.\nSurplus of pro-rata applicants = 10,000 × 60 = Rs. 6,00,000. Allotment due is only 8,000 × 40 = Rs. 3,20,000, so only Rs. 3,20,000 can be adjusted. The other Rs. 2,80,000 is refunded, since there are no calls.\nRefund to rejected applicants = 2,000 × 60 = Rs. 1,20,000.\nTotal refund = 2,80,000 + 1,20,000 = Rs. 4,00,000\nCheck: 4,80,000 + 3,20,000 + 4,00,000 = 12,00,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-084","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":84,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Sun Ltd. has 8,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each maturing during the year ending March 31, 2026. What is the minimum amount it must invest or deposit in specified securities (Debenture Redemption Investment) on or before April 30, 2025?","image":"","options":["Rs. 80,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 8,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: debentures maturing during the year = 8,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 8,00,000.\nRule: on or before April 30, the company must invest at least 15% of the debentures maturing during the year ending on the next March 31.\nSubstituting: 15% × 8,00,000 = Rs. 1,20,000.\nRs. 80,000 (10%) is the DRR rate for other unlisted companies, not the DRI rate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-085","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":85,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Discount on issue of debentures may be written off by debiting Securities Premium Reserve.\nReason (R): Section 52(2) of the Companies Act, 2013 permits Securities Premium Reserve to be applied for writing off discount on issue of debentures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that discount or loss can be written off from Securities Premium Reserve.\nR is true: Section 52(2) is the legal provision that allows this use of the reserve.\nLink: A is allowed precisely because of the provision stated in R. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-086","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":86,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Method of redemption) with List-II (Feature):\nList-I\n(A) Payment in lump sum\n(B) Payment in instalments\n(C) Purchase in open market\n(D) Conversion into shares\nList-II\n(i) Applicable only to convertible debentures\n(ii) Whole amount paid at maturity as per terms of issue\n(iii) Company can redeem at its convenience when it has surplus funds, possibly at a discount\n(iv) Debentures to be redeemed identified by draw of lots","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): under lump sum, the entire amount is paid at maturity.\nB-(iv): under instalments, the debentures redeemed each time are selected by draw of lots.\nC-(iii): purchase in the open market lets the company redeem whenever it has surplus funds, possibly below face value.\nD-(i): NCERT states that conversion applies only to convertible debentures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-087","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":87,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Mehta Ltd. issued 4,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of Rs. 10 each. The amount is payable as follows:\nOn application: Rs. 30\nOn allotment: Rs. 40 (including premium)\nOn first and final call: Rs. 40\nAll money was duly received. What amount will be credited to 10% Debentures A/c in the allotment entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,60,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 1,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: allotment money is Rs. 40 per debenture, and it includes the Rs. 10 premium.\nThe premium goes to Securities Premium Reserve. That leaves 40 − 10 = Rs. 30 per debenture for the Debentures A/c.\nSubstituting: 4,000 × 30 = Rs. 1,20,000 to Debentures A/c, and 4,000 × 10 = Rs. 40,000 to Securities Premium Reserve.\nEntry: Debenture Allotment A/c Dr. 1,60,000, To 10% Debentures A/c 1,20,000, To Securities Premium Reserve A/c 40,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-088","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":88,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"On 1 April 2024, Neel Ltd. issued 20,000, 8% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 8%, redeemable at a premium of 5%. The balance in Securities Premium Reserve on that date was Rs. 1,20,000. The company writes off the loss in the same year. What amount will be debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss?","image":"","options":["Rs. 40,000","Rs. 1,40,000","Rs. 2,60,000","Rs. 1,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 20,000 debentures; discount 8%; premium on redemption 5%; Securities Premium Reserve = Rs. 1,20,000.\nLoss on issue = 20,000 × (8 + 5) = Rs. 2,60,000.\nThe loss is written off first from Securities Premium Reserve, up to its balance of Rs. 1,20,000.\nSo the remainder debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss = 2,60,000 − 1,20,000 = Rs. 1,40,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-089","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":89,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Sunrise Ltd. issued debentures on the following terms:\n• After 3 years, 40% of the face value of each debenture will be converted into equity shares. The remaining 60% will be repaid in cash at the end of 6 years.\n• The names, addresses and holdings of all holders are entered in the company's register. Transfers require a transfer deed.\n• A charge is created specifically on the company's factory land and building.\nWhich combination correctly classifies these debentures?","image":"","options":["Fully convertible, bearer, secured by floating charge","Partly convertible, registered, secured by fixed charge","Partly convertible, registered, unsecured","Non-convertible, registered, secured by floating charge"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Convertibility: only 40% of the value converts into shares, so they are partly convertible.\nRegistration: holder details are kept in a register and transfer needs a deed, so they are registered, not bearer.\nSecurity: the charge is on a specific asset held for use, not for sale, so it is a fixed charge.\nOnly option (2) matches all three features.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-090","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":90,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Arya Ltd. has 4,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each. Interest is payable half-yearly, and tax is deducted at source @ 10%. How much is actually paid to the debentureholders for one half-year?","image":"","options":["Rs. 16,200","Rs. 18,000","Rs. 32,400","Rs. 14,580"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Nominal value = 4,000 × 100 = Rs. 4,00,000; rate 9% p.a.; half-yearly payment; TDS 10%.\nFormula: Half-yearly interest = Nominal value × Rate × 6/12, and Net payment = Interest − TDS.\nSubstituting: Interest = 4,00,000 × 9% × 1/2 = Rs. 18,000. TDS = 10% of 18,000 = Rs. 1,800.\nSo the net amount paid to debentureholders = 18,000 − 1,800 = Rs. 16,200.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-091","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":91,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi Ltd. took over assets of Rs. 5,00,000 and liabilities of Rs. 80,000 from Sohan & Co. The agreed purchase consideration was Rs. 4,00,000, paid by issuing 9% debentures at par. What amount will be credited to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 80,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: assets = Rs. 5,00,000; liabilities = Rs. 80,000; purchase consideration = Rs. 4,00,000.\nFormula: Net assets = Assets − Liabilities = 5,00,000 − 80,000 = Rs. 4,20,000.\nThe consideration (Rs. 4,00,000) is less than the net assets, so the difference goes to Capital Reserve.\nSubstituting: 4,20,000 − 4,00,000 = Rs. 20,000.\nOption (1) wrongly ignores the liabilities taken over.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-092","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":92,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are methods of redemption of debentures listed in NCERT?\n(i) Payment in lump sum\n(ii) Forfeiture of debentures\n(iii) Payment in instalments\n(iv) Purchase in the open market\n(v) Issue of bonus shares to debentureholders\n(vi) Conversion into shares or new debentures","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives four methods of redemption: payment in lump sum, payment in instalments, purchase in the open market, and conversion into shares or new debentures.\nSo items (i), (iii), (iv) and (vi) are valid methods.\nItem (ii) is not a method: forfeiture applies to shares when calls are unpaid.\nItem (v) is not a method either: bonus shares are issued to shareholders, not used to discharge debenture liability.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-093","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":93,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"On 1 July 2023, Nidhi Ltd. issued 50,000, 8% debentures of Rs 100 each at a discount of 8%. They are redeemable after 5 years at a premium of 6%. The company had a balance of Rs 3,00,000 in Securities Premium Reserve. What amount of the loss on issue will be written off from the Statement of Profit and Loss in the year of issue?","image":"","options":["Rs 1,00,000","Rs 4,00,000","Rs 7,00,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 50,000 debentures, discount 8%, premium on redemption 6%, Securities Premium Reserve Rs 3,00,000.\nLoss on issue = discount + premium on redemption = 50,000 × (8 + 6) = Rs 7,00,000.\nWrite-off order: Securities Premium Reserve is used first, absorbing Rs 3,00,000.\nBalance charged to Statement of Profit and Loss = 7,00,000 − 3,00,000 = Rs 4,00,000.\nRs 1,00,000 wrongly ignores the premium on redemption.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-094","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":94,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. bought machinery from Sharma Traders for a purchase consideration of ₹3,30,000. It paid the vendor by issuing 9% debentures of ₹100 each at a premium of 10%. How many debentures will Kiran Ltd. issue to Sharma Traders?","image":"","options":["3,300","3,000","3,630","2,700"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: purchase consideration = ₹3,30,000; face value = ₹100; premium = 10%.\nIssue price = 100 + 10 = ₹110 per debenture.\nFormula: number of debentures = purchase consideration ÷ issue price.\nSubstituting: 3,30,000 ÷ 110 = 3,000.\nOption (1) wrongly divides by the face value of ₹100.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-095","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":95,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a permitted avenue for investing the Debenture Redemption Investment (the 15% amount kept for debentures maturing during the year)?","image":"","options":["Deposit with a scheduled bank, free from any charge or lien","Securities of the Central Government or of a State Government","Equity shares of a listed company purchased through a stock exchange","Bonds issued by a company notified under sub-clause (f) of section 20 of the Indian Trusts Act"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four permitted avenues: a deposit with a scheduled bank free from charge or lien, Central or State Government securities, securities under sub-clauses (a) to (d) and (ee) of section 20 of the Indian Trusts Act, and bonds of a company notified under sub-clause (f) of that section.\nOptions (1), (2) and (4) are on this list.\nEquity shares of a listed company are not on the list. They are risky, and the money must stay safe for repaying debentureholders.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-096","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":96,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Debenture money is payable in two instalments: on application and on allotment. Application money has already been received. Which entry transfers this application money at the time of allotment?","image":"","options":["Bank A/c Dr. To Debenture Application A/c","Debenture Allotment A/c Dr. To Debentures A/c","Debenture Application A/c Dr. To Debentures A/c","Debentures A/c Dr. To Debenture Application A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In a two-instalment issue, application money is first credited to Debenture Application A/c.\nWhen debentures are allotted, the application money becomes part of the debenture liability. So Debenture Application A/c is debited and Debentures A/c is credited.\nOption (1) records the receipt of cash, not the transfer.\nOption (2) records the allotment money due.\nOption (4) reverses the correct entry.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-097","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":97,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company offered debentures to the public, and the issue was over-subscribed. Some applicants received no debentures at all. What must the company do with the application money received from these applicants?","image":"","options":["Keep it and adjust it towards the allotment money of other applicants","Refund it to those applicants","Credit it to Securities Premium Reserve","Transfer it to Capital Reserve"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: in an over-subscribed issue, money received from applicants who are allotted no debentures must be refunded to them.\nOnly applicants who get a pro-rata (partial) allotment can have their excess money retained and adjusted towards allotment and calls.\nOption (1) is tempting, but money of one applicant cannot be adjusted against another applicant's dues.\nOptions (3) and (4) are wrong because this money is not a gain to the company.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-098","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":98,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Delta Ltd. issued 2,000 zero coupon debentures of Rs. 1,000 each at Rs. 650 each. They are redeemable at par after 5 years. The implicit interest is spread equally over the life of the debentures. What is the amount of interest relating to each year?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,40,000","Rs. 7,00,000","Rs. 1,30,000","Rs. 2,60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Face value = Rs. 1,000; issue price = Rs. 650; 2,000 debentures; life = 5 years.\nConcept: For zero coupon debentures, the difference between nominal value and issue price is treated as interest for the duration of the debentures.\nTotal interest = (1,000 − 650) × 2,000 = Rs. 7,00,000.\nPer year = 7,00,000 ÷ 5 = Rs. 1,40,000.\nOption (2) is the total for all 5 years, not one year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-099","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":99,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Debenture is a written instrument acknowledging a debt. It is issued under the ________ of the company.","image":"","options":["Memorandum of Association","Common seal","Certificate of incorporation","Articles of Association"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a debenture as a written instrument acknowledging a debt under the common seal of the company.\nIt contains a contract for repaying the principal and for paying interest at a fixed rate.\nThe Memorandum and Articles are constitutional documents of the company; they are not the authentication under which a debenture is executed.\nThe certificate of incorporation only proves that the company has been formed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-100","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":100,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match the transaction in List-I with the account debited in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Interest falls due on debentures\n(b) Interest paid to debentureholders\n(c) Interest account closed at year end\n(d) TDS deposited with the government\nList-II\n(i) Debentureholders A/c\n(ii) Statement of Profit and Loss\n(iii) Debenture Interest A/c\n(iv) Income Tax Payable A/c","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) When interest is due: Debenture Interest A/c Dr., To Income Tax Payable and To Debentureholders. So (a)-(iii).\n(b) On payment: Debentureholders A/c Dr., To Bank. So (b)-(i).\n(c) At year end: Statement of Profit and Loss Dr., To Debenture Interest. So (c)-(ii).\n(d) On depositing TDS: Income Tax Payable A/c Dr., To Bank. So (d)-(iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-101","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":101,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Zeta Ltd. has 11,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each, redeemable as follows:\nJanuary 31, 2024: 4,000 debentures\nJanuary 31, 2025: 2,000 debentures\nJanuary 31, 2026: 5,000 debentures\nIt keeps Debenture Redemption Investment exactly equal to the legal minimum each year. Any excess is realised at the time of redemption, and investments are not realised in full until the last redemption. What fresh amount must it invest on or before April 30, 2025?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 75,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: For 2024, the required DRI is 15% × 4,00,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nStep 2: For 2025, only 15% × 2,00,000 = Rs. 30,000 is needed. The excess Rs. 30,000 is realised on Jan 31, 2024, leaving Rs. 30,000.\nStep 3: For 2026, the required DRI is 15% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 75,000.\nStep 4: The existing Rs. 30,000 is carried forward, so the fresh investment is 75,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 45,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-102","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":102,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. took over assets of Rs. 5,00,000 and liabilities of Rs. 60,000 from Arun Traders. The agreed purchase consideration was Rs. 4,62,000. It was paid by issuing 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 10%. How many debentures will Kiran Ltd. issue to Arun Traders?","image":"","options":["4,620","4,200","4,400","4,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: purchase consideration = Rs. 4,62,000. Issue price = 100 + 10 = Rs. 110 per debenture.\nFormula: number of debentures = purchase consideration ÷ issue price.\nSubstituting: 4,62,000 ÷ 110 = 4,200.\nNote: net assets are 5,00,000 − 60,000 = Rs. 4,40,000. The excess consideration of Rs. 22,000 is debited to Goodwill.\nDividing by the face value of Rs. 100 gives the wrong figure of 4,620.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-103","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":103,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Sunrise Ltd. offered 5,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each. Rs. 30 was payable on application and Rs. 70 on allotment. Applications were received for 7,000 debentures. Applications for 1,500 debentures were rejected, and the remaining applicants were allotted 5,000 debentures on a pro-rata basis. What amount of application money will be refunded?","image":"","options":["Rs. 45,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 2,10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: rejected applications = 1,500 debentures; application money = Rs. 30 per debenture.\nRule: only rejected applicants get a refund. The excess money of pro-rata applicants (500 × 30) is retained for allotment.\nSubstituting: Refund = 1,500 × 30 = Rs. 45,000\nRs. 60,000 is wrong because it wrongly treats all 2,000 excess debentures as refunded.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-104","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":104,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Term) with List-II (Feature) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\nA. Fixed charge\nB. Floating charge\nC. Zero coupon rate debenture\nD. Irredeemable debenture\nList-II:\n(i) Repayable on winding-up of the company or on expiry of a long period\n(ii) Created on a specific asset held for use in operations and not meant for sale\n(iii) Covers the general assets of the company, excluding those assigned to secured creditors\n(iv) Difference between nominal value and issue price is treated as interest","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(ii): A fixed charge is created on a specific asset held for use in operations, not for sale.\nB-(iii): A floating charge is on the general assets of the company, excluding those assigned to secured creditors.\nC-(iv): Zero coupon debentures carry no interest rate. They are issued at a substantial discount, and the discount serves as the interest.\nD-(i): Irredeemable (perpetual) debentures carry no repayment undertaking. They are repaid on winding-up or after a long period.\nOption (1) swaps the two charges, and options (2) and (4) misplace C or D.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-105","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":105,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Type of debenture) with List-II (Feature):\nList-I\n(A) Bearer debentures\n(B) Zero coupon rate debentures\n(C) Irredeemable debentures\n(D) Registered debentures\nList-II\n(i) Issued at a substantial discount; the difference from nominal value is treated as interest\n(ii) Transferred only by executing a regular transfer deed\n(iii) Transferred by mere delivery; interest is paid to whoever produces the coupon\n(iv) Also called perpetual debentures; repayable on winding up or after a long period","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Bearer debentures pass by delivery, and the company keeps no record of holders, so A matches (iii).\n(B) Zero coupon debentures carry no stated interest rate. They are issued at a deep discount instead, so B matches (i).\n(C) Irredeemable debentures are known as perpetual debentures, so C matches (iv).\n(D) Registered debentures have holders' details entered in a register, and transfer needs a transfer deed, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-106","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":106,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The accountant of Meera Ltd. passed the following entry on September 30 for half-yearly interest on its 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each:\nDebenture Interest A/c Dr. Rs. 25,000\nTo Debentureholders A/c Rs. 22,500\nTo Income Tax Payable A/c Rs. 2,500\nHow many debentures has the company issued?","image":"","options":["5,000","2,500","4,500","10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Gross half-yearly interest = Rs. 25,000 (the debit to Debenture Interest A/c). The Rs. 2,500 is 10% TDS.\nAnnual interest = 25,000 × 2 = Rs. 50,000.\nNominal value = 50,000 ÷ 10% = Rs. 5,00,000.\nNumber of debentures = 5,00,000 ÷ 100 = 5,000.\nThe option 4,500 wrongly uses the net Rs. 22,500. The option 2,500 forgets that the interest is for half a year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-107","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":107,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Meera Ltd. issued 4,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of Rs. 10 per debenture. The amount is payable as follows:\nRs. 30 on application\nRs. 50 on allotment (including premium)\nRs. 30 on first and final call\nWhat amount will be credited to Debentures A/c when allotment money becomes due?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,60,000","Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: allotment money is Rs. 50 per debenture, of which Rs. 10 is premium.\nFace-value part of allotment = 50 − 10 = Rs. 40 per debenture.\nDebentures A/c credit = 4,000 × 40 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nSecurities Premium Reserve is credited with 4,000 × 10 = Rs. 40,000.\nRs. 2,00,000 is the total allotment due, including premium, which is debited to Debenture Allotment A/c.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-108","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":108,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Tejas Ltd. had a balance of Rs. 2,50,000 in Securities Premium Reserve on April 1, 2019. During 2019-20:\n• On July 1, 2019 it issued 1,00,000, 8% debentures of Rs. 50 each at a 4% discount, redeemable after 5 years at a 6% premium.\n• On October 1, 2019 it issued 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 3 per share.\nAll amounts were received in full. What amount of loss on issue of debentures will be debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss for the year ending March 31, 2020?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 2,50,000","Rs. 5,00,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: face value of debentures = 1,00,000 × 50 = Rs. 50,00,000.\nFormula: Loss = discount + premium on redemption = (4% + 6%) × 50,00,000 = Rs. 5,00,000.\nSPR available: 2,50,000 + (50,000 × 3) = Rs. 4,00,000.\nSo the Statement of Profit and Loss is debited with 5,00,000 − 4,00,000 = Rs. 1,00,000.\nWhy Rs. 2,50,000 is wrong: it ignores the share premium received during the year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-109","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":109,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"The word 'debenture' has been derived from the Latin word 'debere'. What does 'debere' mean?","image":"","options":["To own","To borrow","To lend","To secure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: 'debenture' comes from the Latin word 'debere', which means to borrow.\nThis fits because a company issues debentures to borrow long-term funds, so a debenture represents borrowed capital.\n'To lend' is the most tempting wrong option. Lending is what the debentureholder does, but the word describes the company's act of borrowing.\n'To own' describes shares, not debentures.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-110","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":110,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Under the provisions explained in NCERT, which of the following companies must create a Debenture Redemption Reserve (DRR) before redeeming its debentures?","image":"","options":["A banking company","An NBFC registered with the Reserve Bank of India","A company whose shares are listed on a stock exchange","An 'other unlisted' company"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The following are exempt from creating DRR: all India financial institutions regulated by RBI, banking companies, NBFCs registered with RBI, housing finance companies registered with the National Housing Bank, and listed companies.\nOptions (1), (2) and (3) are therefore exempt.\nFor 'other unlisted companies', a DRR equal to 10% of the outstanding debentures is required.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-111","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":111,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When interest is paid to debentureholders, the entry is Debentureholders A/c Dr. To Bank A/c.\nStatement II: Payment of interest on debentures is an appropriation of profits, just like dividend on shares.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct","Both statements are correct","Both statements are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. When interest falls due, Debentureholders A/c is credited. On payment, Debentureholders A/c is debited and Bank A/c is credited.\nStatement II is incorrect. Dividend is an appropriation of profit, but debenture interest is a charge against profit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-112","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":112,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Arya Ltd. purchased assets from Neel Ltd. for a purchase consideration of Rs. 2,64,000. The consideration was paid by issuing 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 10%. How many debentures were issued?","image":"","options":["2,640","2,400","2,200","2,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: purchase consideration = Rs. 2,64,000; face value = Rs. 100; premium = 10%.\nFormula: Number of debentures = Purchase consideration ÷ Issue price.\nIssue price = 100 + 10 = Rs. 110.\nSubstituting: 2,64,000 ÷ 110 = 2,400.\nOption (1) wrongly divides by the face value of Rs. 100.\nSo, 2,400 debentures were issued.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-113","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":113,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company has issued 8% debentures of Rs. 100 each, partly at a discount and partly at a premium. On which amount is the interest on these debentures calculated?","image":"","options":["Nominal (face) value of the debentures","Issue price of the debentures","Redemption value of the debentures","Amount actually received in cash by the company"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: interest on debentures is always calculated at the stated rate on the nominal (face) value.\nThe issue price changes with the discount or premium, but the interest does not.\nRedemption value includes any premium on redemption, so it is also not the base.\nCash actually received is simply the issue price, so it is wrong for the same reason.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-114","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":114,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (transaction) with List-II (journal entry):\nList-I\n(a) Receipt of full issue price on application for debentures issued at par\n(b) Allotment money due on debentures issued at a premium\n(c) Allotment money due on debentures issued at a discount\n(d) Making the first and final call on debentures\nList-II\n(i) Debenture First & Final Call A/c Dr. To Debentures A/c\n(ii) Bank A/c Dr. To Debenture Application & Allotment A/c\n(iii) Debenture Allotment A/c Dr., Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c Dr. To Debentures A/c\n(iv) Debenture Allotment A/c Dr. To Debentures A/c, To Securities Premium Reserve A/c","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) A lump-sum receipt is credited to the combined Debenture Application & Allotment A/c, which is (ii).\n(b) Premium due with allotment is credited to Securities Premium Reserve, which is (iv).\n(c) Discount allowed at allotment is debited to Discount on Issue of Debentures, which is (iii).\n(d) A call made is debited to the Call A/c and credited to Debentures A/c, which is (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-115","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":115,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about bonds is correct as per NCERT?","image":"","options":["Bonds can be issued only by the Government","Bonds represent ownership in the issuing organisation","Bonds are instruments acknowledging a debt, and the terms 'bonds' and 'debentures' are now used interchangeably","Bonds carry voting rights, while debentures do not"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A bond is an instrument that acknowledges a debt. Traditionally, bonds were issued by the Government. Today semi-government and non-governmental organisations also issue them, so option (1) is wrong.\nBonds acknowledge a debt and do not represent ownership, so option (2) is wrong.\nNCERT states that 'debentures' and 'bonds' are now used interchangeably, so option (3) is correct.\nVoting rights belong to shareholders, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-116","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":116,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. issued 5,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at par. The amount is payable Rs. 40 on application and the balance on allotment. The issue was fully subscribed. What amount will be debited to Debenture Allotment A/c when allotment money becomes due?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 5,00,000","Rs. 2,40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 5,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each, issued at par. Application money is Rs. 40.\nAllotment money per debenture = 100 − 40 = Rs. 60.\nSubstituting: 5,000 × 60 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nEntry: Debenture Allotment A/c Dr. 3,00,000, To Debentures A/c 3,00,000.\nSo the allotment money due = Rs. 3,00,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-117","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":117,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A share is a part of owned capital, whereas a debenture is a part of borrowed capital.\n(ii) Like the rate of interest on debentures, the rate of dividend on equity shares is fixed in advance.\n(iii) Shareholders enjoy voting rights, whereas debentureholders normally do not.\n(iv) Shares are not secured by any charge on the assets of the company.\n(v) Normally, the amount of shares is returned to shareholders during the life of the company.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: shares are owned capital and debentures are borrowed capital.\n(ii) Incorrect: the dividend rate may vary from year to year with profits. Only the debenture interest rate is prefixed.\n(iii) Correct: shareholders vote, while debentureholders normally do not.\n(iv) Correct: shares carry no charge on the company's assets.\n(v) Incorrect: share capital is normally not returned during the life of the company.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-118","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":118,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Orbit Ltd. offered 6,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 10%. Rs. 30 was payable on application and Rs. 80 on allotment (including premium). Applications were received for 9,000 debentures. Applications for 1,000 debentures were rejected. Applicants for 8,000 debentures were allotted 6,000 debentures pro-rata. Surplus application money was adjusted towards allotment. What amount was actually received in cash on allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,80,000","Rs. 4,20,000","Rs. 3,90,000","Rs. 3,60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: allotment due = 6,000 × 80 = Rs. 4,80,000. This includes premium of Rs. 60,000, which is credited to Securities Premium Reserve.\nSurplus of pro-rata applicants = (8,000 − 6,000) × 30 = Rs. 60,000. This is adjusted towards allotment.\nCash received = 4,80,000 − 60,000 = Rs. 4,20,000\nRs. 3,90,000 is wrong because it also deducts the rejected applicants' money (1,000 × 30), which is refunded, not adjusted.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-119","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":119,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Mehta Ltd. purchased machinery worth Rs. 1,80,000 from Gupta Ltd. The payment was made by issuing 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 10%. How many debentures were issued to Gupta Ltd.?","image":"","options":["1,800","2,000","2,200","1,620"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: consideration = Rs. 1,80,000; face value = Rs. 100; discount = 10%.\nFormula: Number of debentures = Consideration ÷ Issue price.\nIssue price = 100 − 10 = Rs. 90.\nSubstituting: 1,80,000 ÷ 90 = 2,000.\nOption (1) wrongly uses the face value of Rs. 100.\nSo, 2,000 debentures were issued.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-120","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":120,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation when debentures are issued for consideration other than cash) with List-II (accounting treatment):\nList-I\n(A) Purchase consideration is more than net assets taken over\n(B) Purchase consideration is less than net assets taken over\n(C) Debentures issued to vendor at a premium\n(D) Debentures issued to vendor at a discount\nList-II\n(i) Capital Reserve A/c credited\n(ii) Goodwill A/c debited\n(iii) Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c debited\n(iv) Securities Premium Reserve A/c credited","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Paying more than the net assets are worth means the excess is paid for goodwill, so Goodwill A/c is debited: (ii).\n(B) Paying less than the net assets are worth gives a capital gain, credited to Capital Reserve: (i).\n(C) The premium on issue is credited to Securities Premium Reserve: (iv).\n(D) The shortfall below face value is debited to Discount on Issue of Debentures: (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-121","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":121,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When debenture money is collected in more than two instalments, the company debits Debenture First Call A/c and credits Debentures A/c when the first call is made.\nStatement II: When application money is received, the Debentures A/c is credited directly with the amount received.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Making a call creates an amount receivable, so the entry is Debenture First Call A/c Dr., To Debentures A/c. The cash entry follows when the call money is received.\nStatement II is incorrect. Application money received is first credited to Debenture Application A/c (Bank A/c Dr., To Debenture Application A/c).\nIt is transferred to the Debentures A/c only on allotment.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-122","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":122,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Varun Ltd. issued 20,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a 5% discount, redeemable at an 8% premium. Its Securities Premium Reserve had a balance of Rs. 1,50,000. The full amount was received on application. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited with Rs. 2,60,000.\n(b) Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c is credited with Rs. 1,60,000.\n(c) Bank A/c is debited with Rs. 19,00,000.\n(d) The Statement of Profit and Loss is debited with Rs. 1,10,000 while writing off the loss.\n(e) Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited with Rs. 1,00,000.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Face value of the issue = 20,000 × 100 = Rs. 20,00,000.\n(a) Correct: Loss = (5% + 8%) × 20,00,000 = Rs. 2,60,000.\n(b) Correct: Premium on redemption = 8% × 20,00,000 = Rs. 1,60,000.\n(c) Correct: Cash received = 20,000 × 95 = Rs. 19,00,000.\n(d) Correct: Statement of Profit and Loss = 2,60,000 − 1,50,000 = Rs. 1,10,000.\n(e) Incorrect: Because the debentures are redeemable at a premium, the discount is included in Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c, not a separate discount account.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-123","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":123,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about an 'other unlisted' company that has fully redeemed its debentures.\nAssertion (A): After all the debentures are redeemed, the balance of Debenture Redemption Reserve is transferred to General Reserve.\nReason (R): Once the debenture liability has been discharged, the reserve kept aside for redemption is no longer needed for that purpose.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: A is true. In NCERT's illustration of an 'other unlisted' company, the DRR is created at 10% of outstanding debentures. After redemption, the entry is DRR A/c Dr. To General Reserve.\nStep 2: R is true. The DRR was set aside out of profits only to protect redemption. When the liability is paid off, the restriction ends and the balance becomes a free reserve.\nStep 3: R gives the reason for the transfer, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-124","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":124,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. purchased its own 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each with a total face value of Rs. 30,000 from the open market at Rs. 95 per debenture. It cancelled them immediately. There were no purchase expenses. What is the profit on redemption of debentures?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,500","Rs. 3,000","Rs. 28,500","Rs. 950"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: face value Rs. 30,000, so 300 debentures were bought at Rs. 95 each.\nFormula: Profit = Face value − Price paid.\nSubstituting: price paid = 300 × 95 = Rs. 28,500, so profit = 30,000 − 28,500.\nEntry: Debentures A/c Dr. 30,000; To Bank 28,500; To Profit on Redemption of Debentures 1,500.\nSo profit = Rs. 1,500.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-125","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":125,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Meera Ltd., a listed company, issued 500, 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 5%, redeemable in a lump sum at a premium of 10%. It had invested the required 15% in Debenture Redemption Investment. On the redemption date, it encashes the DRI and pays the debentureholders. What is the net outflow from the bank on that date (amount paid minus DRI encashed)? Ignore interest.","image":"","options":["Rs. 55,000","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 47,500","Rs. 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: face value = 500 × 100 = Rs. 50,000.\nAmount due on redemption = 50,000 + 10% premium = Rs. 55,000. The issue discount does not affect the redemption amount.\nDRI = 15% × 50,000 = Rs. 7,500, which comes back into the bank on encashment.\nNet outflow = 55,000 − 7,500 = Rs. 47,500.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-126","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":126,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (item in an over-subscribed debenture issue) with List-II (accounting treatment).\nList-I:\n(A) Application money on debentures actually allotted\n(B) Surplus application money of pro-rata applicants\n(C) Application money of rejected applicants\n(D) Balance allotment money received in cash\nList-II:\n(i) Credited to Bank A/c\n(ii) Credited to Debentures A/c\n(iii) Credited to Debenture Allotment A/c\n(iv) Debited to Bank A/c","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Application money on allotted debentures is transferred to Debentures A/c.\nB-(iii): Surplus of pro-rata applicants is credited to Debenture Allotment A/c, which reduces the cash still to be collected.\nC-(i): The refund to rejected applicants is a cash outflow, so Bank A/c is credited.\nD-(iv): Allotment money received is debited to Bank A/c, with Debenture Allotment A/c credited.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-127","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":127,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a company takes over a business and the net assets taken over exceed the purchase consideration, the difference is credited to Capital Reserve.\nReason (R): Capital Reserve is a revenue profit that is freely available for distribution as dividend.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. If the purchase consideration is less than the net assets (assets − liabilities), the company gains on the purchase. This gain is credited to Capital Reserve, as shown in the NCERT illustration of A Limited taking over B & Co.\nR is false. A gain arising from purchasing a business is a capital profit, not a revenue profit. It is not available for normal dividend distribution.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-128","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":128,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When debentures are issued as collateral security and the first method is followed, no journal entry is passed in the books of the company.\nReason (R): The issue of debentures as collateral security does not create any liability for the company.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: under the first method, only a note is added below the loan.\nR is true: the real liability is the loan itself. The debentures only secure it and do not create any additional liability.\nBecause no extra liability arises, there is nothing to record. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-129","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":129,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Sanjeevani Ltd. issued 2,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at par, redeemable at a premium of 8% after 5 years. What amount will be debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c at the time of allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 8,000","Rs. 16,000","Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 2,16,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 2,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each, issued at par, redeemable at 8% premium.\nFormula: Loss on Issue = Nominal value × Premium on redemption %. There is no discount here because the issue is at par.\nSubstituting: Rs. 2,00,000 × 8% = Rs. 16,000.\nSo Loss on Issue of Debentures = Rs. 16,000. The same amount is credited to Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-130","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":130,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Under the first method of dealing with debentures issued as collateral security, the company:","image":"","options":["Debits Debenture Suspense A/c and credits Debentures A/c","Debits Bank A/c and credits Debentures A/c","Passes no journal entry and adds a note below the loan in the balance sheet","Debits Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c with the face value of the debentures"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Under the first method, no entry is made in the books because the issue creates no liability.\nInstead, a note is added below the loan item on the liabilities side. For example: 'Bank Loan (secured by issue of debentures as collateral security)'.\nOption (1) is the second method, so it is the tempting wrong choice.\nNo cash is received, so option (2) is wrong. There is no loss involved, so option (4) is also wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-131","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":131,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Debentureholders do not normally enjoy voting rights.\n(ii) Discount on issue of debentures may be written off out of Securities Premium Reserve.\n(iii) Premium on redemption of debentures is shown under 'Reserves and Surplus'.\n(iv) The Companies Act, 2013 does not allow debentures to be issued at a discount of more than 10%.\n(v) Shares can be converted into debentures if the terms of issue so provide.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: voting rights belong to shareholders, not debentureholders.\n(ii) Correct: discount or loss on issue can be written off from Securities Premium Reserve.\n(iii) Incorrect: premium on redemption is a liability, shown under Long-term Borrowings.\n(iv) Incorrect: the Act imposes no restriction on issuing debentures at a discount.\n(v) Incorrect: debentures can be converted into shares, but shares cannot be converted into debentures.\nSo 2 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-132","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":132,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. issued 20,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 6%, redeemable at a premium of 5%. The company had a balance of Rs. 1,50,000 in Securities Premium Reserve. What amount will be debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss while writing off the loss on issue of debentures in the year of issue?","image":"","options":["Rs. 70,000","Rs. 2,20,000","Nil","Rs. 1,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 20,000 debentures, discount Rs. 6 each, redemption premium Rs. 5 each, Securities Premium Reserve = Rs. 1,50,000.\nLoss on issue = (6 + 5) × 20,000 = Rs. 2,20,000. This includes both the discount on issue and the premium on redemption.\nThe loss is first written off from Securities Premium Reserve, up to its balance of Rs. 1,50,000.\nThe balance charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss = 2,20,000 − 1,50,000 = Rs. 70,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-133","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":133,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. issued 5,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 6%. The debentures are redeemable at a premium of 8%. What amount will be debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c at the time of allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 70,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 5,000 debentures of Rs. 100; discount on issue 6%; premium on redemption 8%.\nRule: when debentures are issued at a discount and redeemable at a premium, the discount on issue plus the premium on redemption are both debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c.\nSubstituting: Discount = 5,000 × 6 = Rs. 30,000. Premium = 5,000 × 8 = Rs. 40,000.\nSo Loss on Issue = 30,000 + 40,000 = Rs. 70,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-134","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":134,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about debentures issued as collateral security are correct?\n(i) They are given to the lender in addition to assets already pledged as primary security.\n(ii) The lender may invoke them if the realisable value of the primary security falls short of the loan amount.\n(iii) Under the first method, a Debenture Suspense account is opened.\n(iv) If invoked, the lender may present the debentures for redemption or sell them in the open market.\n(v) Under the second method, the entry is reversed when the loan is repaid.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: collateral is an additional security given besides the primary security.\n(ii) Correct: it is invoked only when the primary security proves insufficient.\n(iii) Incorrect: the first method involves no entry, only a note. The suspense account belongs to the second method.\n(iv) Correct: the lender may present the debentures for redemption or sell them in the open market.\n(v) Correct: a reverse entry cancels the debentures on repayment.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-135","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":135,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Type of debenture) with List-II (Feature):\nList-I\n(A) Zero coupon rate debentures\n(B) Registered debentures\n(C) Convertible debentures\n(D) Secured debentures\nList-II\n(i) A fixed or floating charge is created on the company's assets\n(ii) Can be changed into equity shares or other securities\n(iii) Issued at a substantial discount, with no specified rate of interest\n(iv) Holders' names and addresses are entered in a register kept by the company","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Zero coupon debentures carry no interest rate. The discount on issue compensates the investor, so A matches (iii).\n(B) Registered debentures have full holder details kept in the company's register, so B matches (iv).\n(C) Convertible debentures can be converted into equity shares or other securities, so C matches (ii).\n(D) Secured debentures carry a fixed or floating charge on assets, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-136","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":136,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"When writing off discount on issue of debentures, a company first uses the balance available in which account, as permitted by Section 52(2) of the Companies Act, 2013?","image":"","options":["General Reserve","Capital Reserve","Securities Premium Reserve","Debenture Redemption Reserve"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Section 52(2) allows Securities Premium Reserve to be used for writing off discount or loss on issue of debentures.\nOrder of use: Securities Premium Reserve is debited first, to the extent of its balance. Any remaining amount is debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nWhy (4) fails: Debenture Redemption Reserve is kept for redemption, not for writing off discount.\nWhy (1) and (2) fail: They are not the reserves the Act names for this purpose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-137","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":137,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Vyom Ltd. invited applications for 10,000, 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each. The amount was payable Rs. 30 on application and Rs. 70 on allotment. Applications were received for 15,000 debentures. Applications for 3,000 debentures were rejected and the money refunded. The remaining applicants were allotted 10,000 debentures on a pro-rata basis, and their excess application money was adjusted towards allotment. How much cash will be received on allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 6,40,000","Rs. 7,00,000","Rs. 6,10,000","Rs. 5,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 10,000 debentures allotted; Rs. 70 due on allotment.\nAllotment money due = 10,000 × 70 = Rs. 7,00,000.\nPro-rata applicants applied for 15,000 − 3,000 = 12,000 debentures but received 10,000.\nExcess application money retained = (12,000 − 10,000) × 30 = Rs. 60,000.\nSo cash received on allotment = 7,00,000 − 60,000 = Rs. 6,40,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-138","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":138,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company issues debentures at par but makes them redeemable at a premium. When the debentures are allotted, which account is debited with the amount of premium payable on redemption?","image":"","options":["Securities Premium Reserve A/c","Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c","Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c","Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the premium payable on redemption is a future cost that the company accepts at the time of issue. At allotment it is debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c.\nThe same amount is credited to Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c, which records the liability. So option (4) is the account credited, not debited.\nSecurities Premium Reserve is credited only when debentures are issued above face value. Discount on Issue A/c is used when debentures are issued below par and redeemed at par.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-139","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":139,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kirti Ltd. issued 10,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 6%, redeemable at par. On that date its Securities Premium Reserve showed a balance of Rs. 40,000. How much will be debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss while writing off the discount?","image":"","options":["Rs. 20,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 40,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 10,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each, discount 6%, Securities Premium Reserve (SPR) Rs. 40,000.\nFormula: Discount = face value of issue × discount rate.\nSubstituting: Discount = 10,000 × 100 × 6% = Rs. 60,000.\nSPR covers Rs. 40,000 of this.\nSo the Statement of Profit and Loss is debited with 60,000 − 40,000 = Rs. 20,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-140","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":140,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Tara Ltd. bought land from Vikas Ltd. for a purchase consideration of Rs. 3,30,000. The amount was paid by issuing 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 10%. What amount will be credited to Securities Premium Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 33,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 27,000","Rs. 3,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: consideration = Rs. 3,30,000; issue price = 100 + 10 = Rs. 110.\nStep 1: Number of debentures = 3,30,000 ÷ 110 = 3,000.\nStep 2: Securities premium = 3,000 × Rs. 10 = Rs. 30,000.\nCheck: Debentures A/c credit = 3,000 × 100 = Rs. 3,00,000, and 3,00,000 + 30,000 = 3,30,000.\nOption (1) wrongly takes 10% of the consideration instead of 10% of the face value.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-141","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":141,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Sarthak Ltd. has 8,000 debentures of Rs. 100 each outstanding. 3,000 debentures are to be redeemed on March 31, 2025 and the remaining 5,000 on March 31, 2026. Sarthak Ltd. made the required Debenture Redemption Investment on or before April 30, 2024 and does not realise it after the first redemption. What additional amount must it invest on or before April 30, 2025 to meet the legal requirement?","image":"","options":["Rs. 75,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule: by April 30, at least 15% of the debentures maturing in the year ending next March 31 must be invested.\nInvestment by April 30, 2024 = 15% of 3,00,000 = Rs. 45,000.\nWhen debentures are redeemed in instalments, this investment is carried forward to the next year.\nRequirement by April 30, 2025 = 15% of 5,00,000 = Rs. 75,000.\nAdditional investment = 75,000 − 45,000 = Rs. 30,000.\nRs. 75,000 ignores the investment carried forward. Rs. 50,000 confuses the 10% DRR rule with the 15% investment rule.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-142","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":142,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Primary security\n(B) Collateral security\n(C) Debenture Suspense A/c (debit)\n(D) Debentures A/c Dr. To Debenture Suspense A/c\nList-II\n(i) Entry passed on repayment of the loan\n(ii) Asset first sold by the lender to recover the loan\n(iii) Additional security invoked only if the first falls short\n(iv) Account debited when collateral debentures are recorded","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Primary security is the asset sold first to recover the loan, so A-(ii).\n(B) Collateral is used only if the primary security falls short, so B-(iii).\n(C) Debenture Suspense is debited when collateral debentures are recorded, so C-(iv).\n(D) This reverse entry cancels the debentures on repayment of the loan, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-143","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":143,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Riya Ltd. took a loan of Rs. 6,00,000 from a bank. It issued 7,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each as collateral security and chose to record the issue in its books. The journal entry will be:","image":"","options":["Debenture Suspense A/c Dr. 7,00,000\n To 9% Debentures A/c 7,00,000","Debenture Suspense A/c Dr. 6,00,000\n To 9% Debentures A/c 6,00,000","9% Debentures A/c Dr. 7,00,000\n To Debenture Suspense A/c 7,00,000","Bank A/c Dr. 7,00,000\n To 9% Debentures A/c 7,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Under the second method, debentures issued as collateral are recorded at their face value.\nFace value = 7,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 7,00,000. The loan amount of Rs. 6,00,000 is not used.\nEntry: Debenture Suspense A/c Dr. To 9% Debentures A/c, Rs. 7,00,000.\nOption (3) is the reverse entry passed when the loan is repaid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-144","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":144,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Sapna Ltd. issued 6,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 8%. The full amount was payable on application, and all money was received. On that date the company had a balance of Rs. 30,000 in Securities Premium Reserve. The company writes off the discount in the year of issue, using Securities Premium Reserve first. What amount will be debited to the Statement of Profit and Loss to write off the discount?","image":"","options":["Rs. 18,000","Rs. 48,000","Rs. 30,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 6,000 debentures with a discount of 8% on Rs. 100, which is Rs. 8 each. Securities Premium Reserve is Rs. 30,000.\nTotal discount = 6,000 × 8 = Rs. 48,000.\nSecurities Premium Reserve absorbs Rs. 30,000, its full balance.\nThe remainder is charged to profit: 48,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 18,000.\nEntry: Securities Premium Reserve A/c Dr. 30,000 and Statement of Profit and Loss Dr. 18,000, To Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c 48,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-145","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":145,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (basis of distinction) with List-II (position of debentures):\nList-I\n(a) Ownership\n(b) Return\n(c) Repayment\n(d) Security\nList-II\n(i) Interest at a prefixed rate\n(ii) Acknowledgement of debt; part of borrowed capital\n(iii) Generally carry a fixed or floating charge over the company's assets\n(iv) Repayable on the expiry of a specified period","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Ownership: a debenture only acknowledges a debt and is part of borrowed capital, so (ii).\n(b) Return: debentureholders receive interest at a prefixed rate, so (i).\n(c) Repayment: debentures are generally issued for a specified period and repaid when it expires, so (iv).\n(d) Security: debentures are generally secured by a fixed or floating charge, so (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-146","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":146,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"A company has 10,000 debentures of Rs 100 each, redeemable at par in two lots: 4,000 debentures on 31 March 2024 and 6,000 debentures on 31 March 2025. It invests the minimum amount required by law in Debenture Redemption Investment (DRI) each year on or before 30 April. It carries the existing investment forward when redemption is in instalments. How much additional investment must it make on or before 30 April 2024?","image":"","options":["Rs 15,000","Rs 30,000","Rs 60,000","Rs 90,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule: DRI must be at least 15% of the debentures maturing during the year. For instalment redemption, the existing DRI is carried forward and only the shortfall is invested.\nFor 2023-24: 15% × 4,00,000 = Rs 60,000 invested by 30 April 2023.\nFor 2024-25: required DRI = 15% × 6,00,000 = Rs 90,000.\nAdditional investment = 90,000 − 60,000 = Rs 30,000.\nRs 90,000 wrongly ignores the investment carried forward.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-147","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":147,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Case: On 1 April, Meera Ltd. issued 200, 8% debentures of Rs. 1,000 each at a discount of 5%. The debentures are redeemable after 5 years at a premium of 10%. On that date the company had a balance of Rs. 18,000 in Securities Premium Reserve. The company writes off the loss on issue in the same year, using Securities Premium Reserve to the extent available. What amount will be charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss?","image":"","options":["Rs. 12,000","Rs. 18,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 2,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 200 debentures of Rs. 1,000 each; discount 5%; redemption premium 10%; Securities Premium Reserve Rs. 18,000.\nLoss on Issue = Discount (200 × 50 = Rs. 10,000) + Premium on redemption (200 × 100 = Rs. 20,000) = Rs. 30,000.\nWrite-off: Securities Premium Reserve Dr. 18,000 (full balance); Statement of Profit and Loss Dr. the remainder.\nSo amount charged = 30,000 − 18,000 = Rs. 12,000.\nRs. 2,000 wrongly ignores the discount on issue.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-148","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":148,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding discount or loss on issue of debentures is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["When debentures are redeemable at a premium, the premium payable on redemption is debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c","When debentures are issued at a discount and redeemable at par, the discount is debited to Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c","Loss on issue of debentures is a revenue loss, so it is always charged to the Statement of Profit and Loss before any reserve is used","Any part of the discount or loss not covered by Securities Premium Reserve is written off from the Statement of Profit and Loss"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: The premium payable on redemption is debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c.\n(2) Correct: For an issue at a discount that is redeemable at par, the usual Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c is used.\n(3) Incorrect: The loss is a capital loss, not a revenue loss. Securities Premium Reserve is used first; the Statement of Profit and Loss bears only the shortfall.\n(4) Correct: This is the NCERT treatment of any amount not covered by the reserve.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-149","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":149,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A listed company redeeming its debentures is not required to create a Debenture Redemption Reserve, but it must still invest at least 15% of the debentures maturing during the year in specified securities.\nReason (R): The amount invested as Debenture Redemption Investment cannot be used for any purpose other than redemption of debentures maturing during that year.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Listed companies are exempt from DRR, but the 15% investment applies to companies generally. In NCERT's illustration, a listed company invests in DRI without creating DRR.\nR is true. The invested sum must be used only for redeeming debentures maturing during that year.\nHowever, R only describes a restriction on how the DRI may be used. It does not explain why DRR is exempt while DRI remains compulsory.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-150","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":150,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Unsecured debentures are also known as naked debentures.\nReason (R): Under no circumstances can any charge be created on the company's assets for unsecured debentures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: unsecured debentures carry no specific charge on assets, so they are called naked debentures.\nR is false: NCERT states that a floating charge may be created on these debentures by default.\nSo saying a charge can never arise is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-151","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":151,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Rohan Ltd. issued 1,000, 9% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 5%, redeemable at a premium of 5%. In the journal entry that transfers application and allotment money on allotment, how many of the following accounts are CREDITED?\n(i) 9% Debentures A/c\n(ii) Securities Premium Reserve A/c\n(iii) Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c\n(iv) Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c\n(v) Debenture Application & Allotment A/c","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The entry is: Debenture Application & Allotment A/c Dr. 1,05,000; Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c Dr. 5,000.\nCredits: To 9% Debentures A/c 1,00,000; To Securities Premium Reserve A/c 5,000; To Premium on Redemption of Debentures A/c 5,000.\n(i), (ii) and (iii) are credited. (iv) and (v) are debited.\nCheck: debits total 1,10,000 and credits total 1,10,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-152","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":152,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about debentures issued for consideration other than cash.\nStatement I: When a company purchases assets from a vendor, the entry is Sundry Assets A/c Dr. To Vendor's A/c.\nStatement II: When debentures are issued to the vendor at a premium, the premium is credited to Securities Premium Reserve A/c.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. The assets received are debited, and the vendor is credited because the company owes the vendor the purchase price.\nStatement II: Correct. On issue at a premium, the entry is Vendor Dr. To Debentures A/c (face value) and To Securities Premium Reserve A/c (premium).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-153","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":153,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (transaction) with List-II (accounting treatment).\nList-I:\n(A) Own debentures bought below par and cancelled\n(B) Own debentures bought above par and cancelled\n(C) DRR balance after all debentures are redeemed\n(D) DRI encashed at the time of redemption\nList-II:\n(i) Credited to General Reserve\n(ii) Bank A/c Dr. To Debenture Redemption Investment A/c\n(iii) Profit finally credited to Capital Reserve\n(iv) Loss written off to Statement of Profit and Loss","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(iii): profit on purchase below par goes to Capital Reserve.\n(B)-(iv): loss on purchase above par is debited to Loss on Redemption A/c and written off to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\n(C)-(i): DRR A/c Dr. To General Reserve.\n(D)-(ii): DRI is realised in cash, so Bank A/c Dr. To DRI A/c.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-154","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":154,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Arora Ltd. took over assets of Rs 3,50,000 and liabilities of Rs 50,000 from Mehta & Co. The agreed purchase consideration was Rs 3,15,000. It was paid by issuing 10% debentures of Rs 100 each at a premium of 25%. What amount will be credited to Securities Premium Reserve A/c?","image":"","options":["Rs 15,000","Rs 63,000","Rs 75,000","Rs 78,750"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Purchase consideration Rs 3,15,000. Issue price = 100 + 25 = Rs 125.\nNumber of debentures = 3,15,000 ÷ 125 = 2,520.\nSecurities Premium = 2,520 × 25 = Rs 63,000. Debentures A/c is credited with 2,520 × 100 = Rs 2,52,000.\nNet assets are 3,50,000 − 50,000 = Rs 3,00,000. The excess of Rs 15,000 is Goodwill, not premium.\nRs 78,750 wrongly takes 25% of the whole consideration.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-155","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":155,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. purchased machinery from Sharma Traders for a purchase consideration of Rs. 2,88,000. The amount was paid by issuing 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 4%. How many debentures were issued to Sharma Traders?","image":"","options":["2,880 debentures","3,000 debentures","3,120 debentures","2,770 debentures"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Purchase consideration = Rs. 2,88,000; face value = Rs. 100; discount = 4%.\nFormula: Number of debentures = Purchase consideration ÷ Issue price.\nIssue price = 100 − 4 = Rs. 96.\nSubstituting: 2,88,000 ÷ 96 = 3,000.\nEntry: Sharma Traders Dr. 2,88,000 and Discount on Issue of Debentures Dr. 12,000, To 10% Debentures 3,00,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-156","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":156,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Nirmal Ltd. issued 50,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 6%, redeemable at a premium of 4%. What amount will be debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c at allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 5,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Nominal value = 50,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 50,00,000. Issue premium = 6%. Redemption premium = 4%.\nRule: The premium received on issue (6% = Rs. 3,00,000) is credited to Securities Premium Reserve. It is not set off against the loss.\nFormula: Loss on Issue = Premium on redemption = 4% of nominal value.\nSubstituting: Rs. 50,00,000 × 4% = Rs. 2,00,000.\nTrap: Rs. 1,00,000 wrongly nets the two premiums against each other.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-157","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":157,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When debentures are over-subscribed, the company may retain the surplus application money of applicants given a pro-rata allotment.\nReason (R): A company may allot more debentures than it invited, so that it does not have to refund any money.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the surplus received on over-subscription may be retained and adjusted towards allotment and the calls to be made.\nR is false. A company cannot allot more debentures than it has invited for subscription.\nSince R is false, it cannot explain A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-158","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":158,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kaveri Ltd. issued 1,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 5%, redeemable at a premium of 10%. What amount will be debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Nominal value = 1,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 1,00,000. Discount on issue = 5%. Premium on redemption = 10%.\nFormula: When debentures are issued at a discount and redeemable at a premium, Loss on Issue = Discount on issue + Premium on redemption.\nSubstituting: Discount = Rs. 5,000. Premium on redemption = Rs. 10,000. Loss = Rs. 5,000 + Rs. 10,000.\nSo Loss on Issue of Debentures = Rs. 15,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-159","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":159,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Zenith Ltd. obtained a bank loan and gave its own debentures to the bank as collateral security. The company records this issue in its books using the second method. Which journal entry will it pass at the time of issuing these debentures?","image":"","options":["Debenture Suspense A/c Dr.\nTo Debentures A/c","Bank A/c Dr.\nTo Debentures A/c","Debentures A/c Dr.\nTo Debenture Suspense A/c","Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c Dr.\nTo Debentures A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Debentures issued as collateral security bring in no cash and create no new liability. Under the second method, the issue is still recorded so that it appears in the books.\nThe entry is: Debenture Suspense A/c Dr. To Debentures A/c, with the nominal value of the debentures.\nOption (3) is the reverse entry. It is passed only when the loan is repaid and the debentures are cancelled.\nBank A/c is not debited because no money is received for these debentures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-160","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":160,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (terms of issue and redemption of debentures) with List-II (treatment at the time of issue).\nList-I:\n(A) Issued at a discount, redeemable at par\n(B) Issued at par, redeemable at a premium\n(C) Issued at a discount, redeemable at a premium\n(D) Issued at a premium, redeemable at par\nList-II:\n(i) No discount or loss arises; Securities Premium Reserve is credited\n(ii) Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited with the discount\n(iii) Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited with the premium on redemption only\n(iv) Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c is debited with the discount plus the premium on redemption","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(ii): An issue at a discount that is redeemable at par uses Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c.\n(B)-(iii): For an issue at par, only the future premium on redemption becomes a loss.\n(C)-(iv): Both the discount on issue and the premium on redemption are debited to Loss on Issue of Debentures A/c.\n(D)-(i): An issue at a premium creates no loss; the premium is credited to Securities Premium Reserve.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-161","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":161,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"As per NCERT, discount or loss on issue of debentures is treated as which of the following?","image":"","options":["A revenue loss, written off over the life of the debentures","A capital loss, written off in the year in which the debentures are issued","A current liability, shown under short-term provisions","A deferred revenue expense, carried forward indefinitely"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Discount or loss on issue of debentures is a capital loss. It is written off in the year the debentures are issued.\nWhy (2) fits: It states both the nature (capital loss) and the timing (year of issue).\nWhy (1) fails: It calls the item a revenue loss, which is wrong.\nWhy (3) fails: The item is not a liability.\nWhy (4) fails: The item is written off, not carried forward indefinitely.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-162","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":162,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A company must pay interest on its debentures even in a year in which it has suffered a loss.\nReason (R): Payment of interest on debentures is a charge on profits, whereas payment of dividend is an appropriation of profits.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: debenture interest is payable whether or not the company earns a profit.\nR is true: interest is a charge on profits, while dividend is an appropriation of profits.\nBecause interest is a charge, it does not depend on the availability of profit. That is exactly why it must be paid even in a loss year, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-163","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":163,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When debentures are issued at a discount and payable in instalments, Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c is usually debited at the time allotment money becomes due.\nStatement II: Discount on issue of debentures may be written off out of Securities Premium Reserve, if such a balance exists.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In the NCERT treatment, the allotment entry is: Debenture Allotment A/c Dr., Discount on Issue of Debentures A/c Dr., To Debentures A/c. This entry records the full face value.\nStatement II is correct. Discount can be written off by debiting Securities Premium Reserve to the extent of its balance. Any remainder is written off to the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-DEBENTURES-164","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","qno":164,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Issue and Redemption of Debentures","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. issued debentures with interest coupons attached. The company keeps no record of who holds them, they can be transferred simply by handing them over, and interest is paid to whoever presents the coupon. Which type of debentures has Kavya Ltd. issued?","image":"","options":["Registered debentures","Bearer debentures","Perpetual debentures","Zero coupon rate debentures"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Classification by registration: debentures are either registered or bearer.\nBearer debentures pass by mere delivery. The company keeps no record of the holders, and interest is paid to the person who produces the interest coupon. This matches the case exactly.\nRegistered debentures are tempting, but the company keeps a register of holders' names and addresses, and transfer needs a regular transfer deed.\nPerpetual debentures relate to tenure, not transferability.\nZero coupon debentures carry no coupon rate at all.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Issue and Redemption of Debentures","difficulty":"easy"}]});
